window.PGET_EXAM = {"id":"paper1","title":"PGET Paper I — Basic Medical Sciences","short":"Paper I · Basic Sciences","subtitle":"Mock exam built on the UHS Table of Specifications for MBBS graduates","durationMin":120,"questions":[{"id":1,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"General Anatomy","domain":"knowledge","question":"During late pregnancy, relaxin softens a midline joint of the pelvis in which the articular surfaces are covered by thin plates of hyaline cartilage united by a disc of fibrocartilage. This joint is best classified as a:","options":["Primary cartilaginous joint (synchondrosis)","Secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis)","Fibrous joint (syndesmosis)","Plane synovial joint","Gomphosis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pubic symphysis is a secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis): hyaline cartilage covers the bone ends, which are joined by a fibrocartilaginous disc. Symphyses lie in the midline and are permanent (pubic symphysis, intervertebral discs, manubriosternal joint). Primary cartilaginous joints (synchondroses) are hyaline-only and mostly temporary.","why_wrong":{"0":"Synchondroses (e.g. epiphyseal plate, first sternocostal joint) are united by hyaline cartilage alone and are usually temporary.","2":"Syndesmoses (e.g. inferior tibiofibular joint) are united by fibrous tissue/interosseous membrane, not a fibrocartilaginous disc.","3":"Synovial joints have a joint cavity, synovial membrane and capsule, which the pubic symphysis lacks.","4":"A gomphosis is the fibrous peg-and-socket joint between a tooth and its alveolus."}},{"id":2,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"General Anatomy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 28-year-old man presents with a painless, firm swelling of the right testis. Orchidectomy confirms seminoma. To which group of lymph nodes does this tumour first metastasize?","options":["Para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes","Superficial inguinal lymph nodes","Deep inguinal lymph nodes","Internal iliac lymph nodes","External iliac lymph nodes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The testis develops on the posterior abdominal wall and descends, taking its vessels and lymphatics with it; testicular lymph therefore runs with the testicular vessels to the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes at about L1-L2. In contrast, scrotal skin drains to the superficial inguinal nodes, so palpable inguinal nodes suggest scrotal rather than testicular disease.","why_wrong":{"1":"Superficial inguinal nodes drain the scrotal skin, not the testis.","2":"Deep inguinal nodes drain the glans penis and lower limb deep tissues, not the testis.","3":"Internal iliac nodes drain pelvic viscera such as the prostate and bladder base, not the testis.","4":"External iliac nodes receive lymph from the inguinal nodes and some pelvic organs but are not the primary testicular drainage."}},{"id":3,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Embryology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A newborn has nasal hypoplasia and stippled epiphyses on X-ray. The mother had a mechanical mitral valve and took an oral medication throughout the first trimester. Which drug is most likely responsible?","options":["Warfarin","Phenytoin","Sodium valproate","Lithium","Isotretinoin"],"answer":0,"explanation":"First-trimester exposure to warfarin (needed for mechanical valves) causes fetal warfarin syndrome, characterised by nasal hypoplasia and stippled (chondrodysplasia punctata-like) epiphyses; later exposure risks fetal CNS haemorrhage. Warfarin crosses the placenta, whereas heparins do not.","why_wrong":{"1":"Phenytoin causes fetal hydantoin syndrome: craniofacial dysmorphism, hypoplastic nails/distal phalanges and growth retardation.","2":"Valproate is chiefly associated with neural tube defects (spina bifida).","3":"Lithium is associated with Ebstein anomaly of the tricuspid valve.","4":"Isotretinoin causes retinoic acid embryopathy: ear, craniofacial, cardiac and CNS defects."}},{"id":4,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Embryology","domain":"knowledge","question":"Following fusion of a sperm with the secondary oocyte in the ampulla of the uterine tube, cortical granules release their contents. Which event does this directly bring about?","options":["Block to polyspermy by alteration of the zona pellucida","Penetration of the corona radiata by the sperm","Capacitation of the sperm","Release of hyaluronidase and acrosin from the sperm acrosome","Compaction of the morula"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When the sperm fuses with the oocyte membrane, cortical granules release lysosomal enzymes (cortical and zona reactions) that make the oocyte membrane and zona pellucida impenetrable to other sperm, preventing polyspermy. Fusion also triggers completion of the second meiotic division of the oocyte (Langman).","why_wrong":{"1":"Corona radiata penetration is achieved by the capacitated sperm and precedes fusion with the oocyte.","2":"Capacitation occurs in the female genital tract before the sperm reaches the oocyte.","3":"The acrosome reaction is a sperm event triggered by binding to the zona, not by cortical granules.","4":"Compaction occurs after the 8-cell stage of cleavage, days later."}},{"id":5,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Embryology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A newborn girl has a large mass at the base of the spine. Histology shows tissues derived from all three germ layers, including hair, cartilage and gut epithelium. Persistence of which embryonic structure most likely gave rise to this tumour?","options":["Primitive streak","Notochord","Neural crest","Allantois","Cloacal membrane"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The primitive streak appears in the third week and normally regresses; remnants in the sacrococcygeal region may form a sacrococcygeal teratoma, which contains tissues from ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm because the streak cells are pluripotent. It is the most common tumour in newborns and is more frequent in girls (Langman).","why_wrong":{"1":"Notochord remnants give rise to chordoma, not a tumour with all three germ layers.","2":"Neural crest tumours (e.g. neuroblastoma) contain neural crest derivatives only.","3":"Allantoic remnants form the urachus; persistence causes urachal fistula or cyst.","4":"Cloacal membrane defects cause anorectal or urogenital malformations, not a teratoma."}},{"id":6,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Histology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 75-year-old woman has tense, non-rupturing blisters on her flexures. Direct immunofluorescence shows linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane. The autoantibodies target which junctional structure?","options":["Desmosome (macula adherens)","Hemidesmosome","Zonula occludens","Zonula adherens","Gap junction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bullous pemphigoid is caused by autoantibodies against hemidesmosomal proteins (BP180/BP230), which anchor basal keratinocytes to the basement membrane, producing subepidermal tense blisters with linear immunofluorescence. Pemphigus vulgaris, in contrast, targets desmosomes (desmoglein 3) and gives intraepidermal flaccid blisters with a net-like pattern.","why_wrong":{"0":"Antibodies to desmoglein in desmosomes cause pemphigus vulgaris with flaccid, easily ruptured intraepidermal blisters.","2":"Tight junctions seal the paracellular space and are not the target in bullous pemphigoid.","3":"Zonula adherens links actin filaments of adjacent cells via cadherins and is not involved here.","4":"Gap junctions (connexons) allow intercellular communication and are not targeted in blistering disease."}},{"id":7,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Histology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A section of cartilage stained with orcein shows chondrocytes in lacunae surrounded by a dense network of branching dark fibres in addition to type II collagen. From which site was this specimen most likely taken?","options":["Thyroid cartilage","Intervertebral disc","Epiglottis","Articular surface of the femur","Costal cartilage"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Elastic cartilage contains a network of elastic fibres (demonstrated with orcein or Verhoeff stain) plus type II collagen; it is found in the epiglottis, external ear (auricle), external acoustic meatus, auditory tube and the corniculate/cuneiform cartilages. The thyroid, cricoid and most arytenoid cartilage is hyaline.","why_wrong":{"0":"Thyroid cartilage is hyaline cartilage, which lacks abundant elastic fibres.","1":"The annulus fibrosus is fibrocartilage, rich in type I collagen bundles, not elastic fibres.","3":"Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage without perichondrium or elastic fibres.","4":"Costal cartilage is hyaline cartilage, which may calcify with age."}},{"id":8,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","domain":"knowledge","question":"Following a difficult breech delivery in which the baby's arm was pulled upward above the head, the infant has a claw-like hand with paralysis of the intrinsic hand muscles, together with ptosis and miosis on the same side. Which part of the brachial plexus is injured?","options":["Upper trunk (C5-C6)","Lower trunk (C8-T1)","Middle trunk (C7)","Posterior cord","Lateral cord"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hyperabduction of the arm tears the lower trunk (C8-T1), producing Klumpke palsy: paralysis of intrinsic hand muscles (claw hand) and sensory loss along the medial forearm and hand. Involvement of T1 white ramus communicans (sympathetic outflow) causes ipsilateral Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis).","why_wrong":{"0":"Upper trunk injury (Erb palsy) from excessive head-shoulder separation gives the 'waiter's tip' posture.","2":"Isolated middle trunk injury is rare and does not cause intrinsic hand paralysis with Horner syndrome.","3":"Posterior cord injury affects radial and axillary nerves, causing wrist drop and deltoid weakness.","4":"Lateral cord injury affects the musculocutaneous nerve and lateral root of the median nerve, not T1."}},{"id":9,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","domain":"knowledge","question":"Two weeks after a modified radical mastectomy with axillary clearance, a woman notices that when she pushes against a wall, the medial border and inferior angle of her scapula stand out from the chest wall. Which nerve was injured?","options":["Thoracodorsal nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Axillary nerve","Long thoracic nerve"],"answer":4,"explanation":"The long thoracic nerve (C5-C7) runs on the superficial surface of serratus anterior along the medial wall of the axilla and is vulnerable in axillary surgery. Paralysis of serratus anterior causes 'winging' of the scapula, most evident when pushing against a wall, and difficulty raising the arm above the head.","why_wrong":{"0":"Thoracodorsal nerve injury weakens latissimus dorsi (extension/adduction of arm) without winging.","1":"Dorsal scapular nerve supplies rhomboids and levator scapulae and is not at risk in axillary clearance.","2":"Suprascapular nerve injury weakens supraspinatus and infraspinatus (abduction initiation, lateral rotation).","3":"Axillary nerve injury causes deltoid paralysis and loss of sensation over the regimental badge area."}},{"id":10,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old motorcyclist sustains a fracture of the neck of the fibula. He now walks with a high-stepping gait, cannot dorsiflex or evert the foot, and has sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot. Which nerve is injured?","options":["Tibial nerve","Femoral nerve","Common fibular (peroneal) nerve","Obturator nerve","Superficial fibular (peroneal) nerve"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The common fibular nerve winds superficially around the neck of the fibula, making it the most commonly injured nerve in the lower limb. Loss of the deep branch (dorsiflexors) and superficial branch (evertors) gives foot drop with a high-stepping gait, and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot and lateral leg.","why_wrong":{"0":"Tibial nerve injury causes loss of plantarflexion and inversion with sensory loss on the sole.","1":"Femoral nerve injury weakens knee extension and abolishes the knee jerk.","3":"Obturator nerve injury weakens thigh adduction with medial thigh sensory loss.","4":"Superficial fibular nerve injury weakens eversion and numbs the dorsum of the foot but spares dorsiflexion, so there is no foot drop."}},{"id":11,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 40-year-old man is asked to stand on his right leg only; the left side of his pelvis sags downward. Which nerve is most likely damaged?","options":["Left superior gluteal nerve","Right superior gluteal nerve","Right inferior gluteal nerve","Left inferior gluteal nerve","Right obturator nerve"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gluteus medius and minimus (superior gluteal nerve, L4-S1) of the stance limb abduct the hip to keep the opposite side of the pelvis level. In a positive Trendelenburg sign the pelvis drops on the unsupported side, so the lesion is on the side of the stance (weight-bearing) limb - here the right superior gluteal nerve.","why_wrong":{"0":"A left-sided lesion would cause the right pelvis to sag when standing on the left leg.","2":"Inferior gluteal nerve supplies gluteus maximus; its injury causes difficulty rising from sitting or climbing stairs.","3":"Gluteus maximus weakness does not produce a positive Trendelenburg sign.","4":"Obturator nerve injury weakens thigh adductors, not the hip abductors that stabilize the pelvis."}},{"id":12,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Head & Neck","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 22-year-old man squeezes an infected pimple at the side of his nose. Three days later he develops fever, proptosis, chemosis and ophthalmoplegia. Through which vein did the infection most likely spread to the cavernous sinus?","options":["Retromandibular vein","External jugular vein","Mastoid emissary vein","Superficial temporal vein","Superior ophthalmic vein"],"answer":4,"explanation":"The facial vein in the 'danger area' (upper lip, nose, medial angle of eye) communicates with the cavernous sinus via the superior ophthalmic vein (and via the deep facial vein-pterygoid plexus). These veins lack effective valves, so squeezing infected lesions can cause septic cavernous sinus thrombosis with proptosis and palsies of CN III, IV and VI.","why_wrong":{"0":"The retromandibular vein drains to the external and internal jugular veins, away from the cavernous sinus.","1":"The external jugular vein drains into the subclavian vein and has no direct link with the cavernous sinus.","2":"The mastoid emissary vein links the occipital/posterior auricular veins with the sigmoid sinus.","3":"The superficial temporal vein joins the maxillary vein to form the retromandibular vein."}},{"id":13,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Head & Neck","domain":"knowledge","question":"A patient with a lesion of the motor root of the right trigeminal nerve is asked to open his mouth widely. The mandible deviates to the right. Paralysis of which muscle mainly explains this deviation?","options":["Left lateral pterygoid","Right masseter","Right temporalis","Right lateral pterygoid","Right medial pterygoid"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The lateral pterygoid pulls the mandibular head and articular disc forward onto the articular tubercle and is the principal muscle initiating mouth opening and protrusion. With unilateral paralysis, the normal opposite lateral pterygoid protrudes its side unopposed, so the chin deviates toward the side of the lesion.","why_wrong":{"0":"The intact left lateral pterygoid is the muscle that pushes the jaw toward the paralysed right side.","1":"Masseter elevates the mandible (closes the mouth) and does not cause deviation on opening.","2":"Temporalis elevates and retracts the mandible; its paralysis does not cause deviation on opening.","4":"Medial pterygoid mainly elevates the mandible and contributes little to opening."}},{"id":14,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Thorax, Abdomen and Pelvis","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 58-year-old man presents with an acute inferior wall myocardial infarction (ST elevation in II, III and aVF). His ECG also shows complete heart block. Occlusion of which artery is most likely?","options":["Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex artery","Left main coronary artery","Diagonal branch of LAD","Right coronary artery"],"answer":4,"explanation":"The right coronary artery supplies the inferior (diaphragmatic) wall in most people (right dominance) and gives the AV nodal artery in about 80-90% of hearts, as well as the SA nodal artery in about 60%. Hence inferior MI is classically associated with bradyarrhythmias and AV block.","why_wrong":{"0":"LAD occlusion causes anterior/anteroseptal infarction (V1-V4).","1":"Circumflex occlusion typically causes lateral (I, aVL, V5-V6) or posterior infarction and supplies the AV node in only a minority.","2":"Left main occlusion causes extensive anterolateral ischaemia and often cardiogenic shock.","3":"Diagonal branches supply the anterolateral wall, not the inferior wall or AV node."}},{"id":15,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Thorax, Abdomen and Pelvis","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 45-year-old man with known peptic ulcer disease presents with massive haematemesis and melaena. Endoscopy reveals a bleeding ulcer on the posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum. Which artery has most likely been eroded?","options":["Left gastric artery","Splenic artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Right gastro-omental artery","Gastroduodenal artery"],"answer":4,"explanation":"The gastroduodenal artery (from the common hepatic artery) descends directly behind the first part of the duodenum, so a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode it and cause severe haemorrhage. Anterior duodenal ulcers tend to perforate into the peritoneal cavity instead.","why_wrong":{"0":"Left gastric artery erosion is associated with ulcers on the lesser curvature of the stomach.","1":"Splenic artery erosion occurs with posterior gastric ulcers penetrating toward the pancreas body.","2":"The SMA lies behind the neck of the pancreas and third part of duodenum, not behind the first part.","3":"The right gastro-omental artery runs along the greater curvature, not posterior to the duodenal bulb."}},{"id":16,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Thorax, Abdomen and Pelvis","domain":"knowledge","question":"A primigravida in the second stage of labour is to receive a transvaginal pudendal nerve block before an outlet forceps delivery. Toward which bony landmark should the needle be directed?","options":["Ischial spine","Ischial tuberosity","Pubic tubercle","Sacral promontory","Coccyx tip"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, crosses the sacrospinous ligament just medial to the ischial spine and re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen into the pudendal canal. The palpable ischial spine therefore guides the needle for pudendal block, which anaesthetises most of the perineum.","why_wrong":{"1":"The ischial tuberosity is the landmark for the sitting bone and hamstring origin, not for the pudendal nerve.","2":"The pubic tubercle is a landmark for the inguinal ligament and hernias.","3":"The sacral promontory is a landmark for measuring the obstetric conjugate.","4":"The coccyx tip is not related to the course of the pudendal nerve."}},{"id":17,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Neuroanatomy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 25-year-old man sustains a stab wound that hemisects the right half of the spinal cord at the T10 level. Which finding is expected?","options":["Loss of pain and temperature sensation in the left leg","Loss of vibration sense in the left leg","Flaccid paralysis with fasciculations of the right leg","Loss of pain and temperature sensation in the right leg","Loss of proprioception in both legs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Brown-Sequard syndrome there is ipsilateral spastic (UMN) paralysis and ipsilateral loss of proprioception, vibration and fine touch (dorsal column) below the lesion, with contralateral loss of pain and temperature (lateral spinothalamic tract) beginning 1-2 segments below the lesion. LMN signs are limited to the segment of the lesion itself.","why_wrong":{"1":"Vibration travels in the ipsilateral dorsal columns, so it is lost on the right, not the left.","2":"The leg is supplied by segments below the lesion, so it shows upper motor neuron (spastic) paralysis.","3":"Spinothalamic fibres cross within 1-2 segments of entry, so pain and temperature are lost contralaterally.","4":"Proprioception is lost only ipsilaterally (right) because only one dorsal column is damaged."}},{"id":18,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Neuroanatomy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 65-year-old hypertensive woman develops sudden weakness and sensory loss of the right lower limb, with only mild weakness of the right arm and a normal face. Speech is normal. Occlusion of which artery is most likely?","options":["Left middle cerebral artery","Left anterior cerebral artery","Right anterior cerebral artery","Left posterior cerebral artery","Basilar artery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The leg and foot are represented on the medial surface of the hemisphere (paracentral lobule), which is supplied by the anterior cerebral artery; the face and arm areas on the lateral surface are supplied by the MCA. Therefore ACA occlusion causes contralateral leg-predominant weakness and sensory loss.","why_wrong":{"0":"Left MCA occlusion affects face and arm more than leg and usually causes aphasia.","2":"Right ACA occlusion would produce left (contralateral) leg weakness.","3":"Left PCA occlusion causes contralateral (right) homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing.","4":"Basilar occlusion causes brainstem signs, quadriparesis, coma or locked-in syndrome."}},{"id":19,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Neuroanatomy","domain":"knowledge","question":"Postganglionic sympathetic fibres to most effectors release noradrenaline. Postganglionic sympathetic fibres supplying which of the following release acetylcholine?","options":["Sinoatrial node","Dilator pupillae","Arrector pili muscles","Eccrine sweat glands","Juxtaglomerular cells"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Eccrine (thermoregulatory) sweat glands are innervated by sympathetic postganglionic fibres that are cholinergic, acting on muscarinic (M3) receptors; hence anticholinergic poisoning causes dry, hot skin. The adrenal medulla is another exception, being innervated directly by preganglionic cholinergic fibres.","why_wrong":{"0":"Sympathetic fibres to the SA node release noradrenaline acting on beta-1 receptors.","1":"Sympathetic fibres to dilator pupillae release noradrenaline acting on alpha-1 receptors.","2":"Arrector pili receive noradrenergic sympathetic fibres acting on alpha-1 receptors.","4":"Juxtaglomerular cells receive noradrenergic fibres; beta-1 stimulation releases renin."}},{"id":20,"subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Neuroanatomy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 3-month-old infant has an enlarging head circumference. MRI shows marked dilatation of both lateral ventricles and the third ventricle, while the fourth ventricle is of normal size. Where is the obstruction most likely located?","options":["Interventricular foramen of Monro","Median aperture (foramen of Magendie)","Arachnoid granulations","Cerebral aqueduct","Lateral apertures (foramina of Luschka)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CSF flows from the lateral ventricles through the interventricular foramina into the third ventricle, then through the cerebral aqueduct into the fourth ventricle and out via the foramina of Magendie and Luschka. Dilated lateral and third ventricles with a normal fourth ventricle localise the block to the aqueduct; aqueductal stenosis is the commonest cause of congenital obstructive hydrocephalus.","why_wrong":{"0":"Obstruction at a foramen of Monro dilates only the corresponding lateral ventricle, sparing the third ventricle.","1":"Obstruction of the fourth ventricle outlets would dilate the fourth ventricle as well.","2":"Impaired absorption causes communicating hydrocephalus with dilatation of all four ventricles.","4":"Outlet obstruction of the fourth ventricle would enlarge the fourth ventricle too."}},{"id":21,"subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Bacteriology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A post-operative wound infection grows Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to cloxacillin and all other beta-lactam antibiotics, including those with beta-lactamase inhibitors. What is the mechanism of this resistance?","options":["Production of plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase","Active efflux of the antibiotic","Altered penicillin-binding protein (PBP2a)","Loss of outer membrane porins","Substitution of D-Ala-D-Ala by D-Ala-D-Lac"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Methicillin-resistant S. aureus (MRSA) carries the mecA gene (on SCCmec), which encodes PBP2a, a penicillin-binding protein with low affinity for beta-lactams. Cell-wall synthesis therefore continues despite the drug, conferring resistance to virtually all beta-lactams; vancomycin or linezolid is used instead.","why_wrong":{"0":"Beta-lactamase is inactive against cloxacillin and is overcome by beta-lactamase inhibitors.","1":"Efflux pumps are a typical resistance mechanism for tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones, not MRSA.","3":"Staphylococci are Gram-positive and lack the outer membrane porins that mediate this mechanism in Gram-negatives.","4":"D-Ala-D-Lac substitution confers vancomycin resistance (e.g. VRE), not methicillin resistance."}},{"id":22,"subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Bacteriology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 7-day-old baby, delivered at home where the umbilical cord was cut with an unsterilized blade, presents with inability to suck, trismus and generalized spasms triggered by touch. The toxin responsible acts by:","options":["Blocking release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor-2","Blocking release of glycine and GABA from inhibitory interneurons","Activating adenylate cyclase by ADP-ribosylation of the Gs protein","Cross-linking MHC class II with T-cell receptors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Neonatal tetanus results from Clostridium tetani spores contaminating the umbilical stump. Tetanospasmin travels by retrograde axonal transport to the spinal cord and, as a zinc metalloprotease, cleaves synaptobrevin, blocking release of the inhibitory neurotransmitters glycine and GABA from Renshaw cells and interneurons, causing spastic paralysis, trismus and spasms.","why_wrong":{"0":"Inhibition of ACh release causes the flaccid paralysis of botulism.","1":"Inactivation of EF-2 is the mechanism of diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A.","3":"Gs activation with raised cAMP is the mechanism of cholera toxin and heat-labile E. coli toxin.","4":"Superantigen activity is the mechanism of TSST-1 and staphylococcal enterotoxins."}},{"id":23,"subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Bacteriology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 24-year-old man has purulent urethral discharge. Gram stain shows Gram-negative intracellular diplococci within neutrophils. Which selective medium is most appropriate to culture this organism from the specimen?","options":["Lowenstein-Jensen medium","Thayer-Martin medium","Thiosulfate-citrate-bile salts-sucrose agar","Bordet-Gengou medium","Loeffler serum slope"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae from contaminated sites is cultured on Thayer-Martin medium, a chocolate agar containing vancomycin, colistin, nystatin (and trimethoprim) to suppress normal flora, incubated in 5-10% CO2. Gram-negative intracellular diplococci in a male urethral smear are highly suggestive of gonorrhoea.","why_wrong":{"0":"Lowenstein-Jensen medium is used to culture Mycobacterium tuberculosis.","2":"TCBS agar is selective for Vibrio cholerae.","3":"Bordet-Gengou (potato-blood-glycerol) agar is used for Bordetella pertussis.","4":"Loeffler serum slope is used for rapid growth of Corynebacterium diphtheriae."}},{"id":24,"subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Virology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old health worker has the following hepatitis B serology: HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, total anti-HBc positive (IgG), IgM anti-HBc negative. What is the correct interpretation?","options":["Immunity due to hepatitis B vaccination","Acute hepatitis B infection","Chronic hepatitis B carrier state","Immunity due to past natural infection","Window period of acute infection"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Anti-HBc develops only after exposure to the whole virus, so the combination of anti-HBs and IgG anti-HBc with negative HBsAg indicates recovery from past infection with immunity. A vaccinated person has anti-HBs alone, because the recombinant vaccine contains only HBsAg.","why_wrong":{"0":"Vaccination produces anti-HBs only; anti-HBc is absent because the vaccine contains HBsAg alone.","1":"Acute infection shows HBsAg positivity with IgM anti-HBc.","2":"Chronic carriers are HBsAg positive for more than 6 months and usually anti-HBs negative.","4":"In the window period HBsAg and anti-HBs are both negative and IgM anti-HBc is positive."}},{"id":25,"subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Virology","domain":"knowledge","question":"Certain herpesviruses downregulate MHC class I molecules on the surface of infected cells to evade cytotoxic T lymphocytes. Which immune cell is specifically activated to kill cells that have lost MHC class I expression?","options":["CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocyte","CD4+ helper T lymphocyte","B lymphocyte","Mast cell","Natural killer cell"],"answer":4,"explanation":"Natural killer cells carry inhibitory receptors (e.g. KIRs) that recognize self MHC class I; when a virus-infected or tumour cell loses MHC I ('missing self'), inhibition is lost and the NK cell kills it with perforin and granzymes. NK activity is enhanced by interferon-alpha/beta and IL-12, making it an early innate antiviral defence.","why_wrong":{"0":"CD8+ T cells require MHC class I-peptide complexes to recognize infected cells, so they are evaded.","1":"CD4+ T cells recognize antigen on MHC class II and do not directly kill MHC-I-deficient cells.","2":"B cells produce antibody and do not kill cells on the basis of absent MHC class I.","3":"Mast cells mediate IgE-dependent hypersensitivity and are not cytotoxic to virus-infected cells."}},{"id":26,"subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Parasitology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 32-year-old man from Karachi was treated with a full course of chloroquine for Plasmodium vivax malaria and became afebrile with a negative smear. Four months later he develops a fresh episode of P. vivax malaria without leaving the city during the low-transmission season. Which parasite stage is most likely responsible for this relapse?","options":["Persisting erythrocytic schizonts","Circulating gametocytes","Sporozoites in the bloodstream","Sequestered schizonts in deep capillaries","Dormant hypnozoites in hepatocytes"],"answer":4,"explanation":"P. vivax and P. ovale form hypnozoites, dormant liver stages that can reactivate weeks to months later and cause relapse. Chloroquine kills only blood schizonts, so primaquine (after G6PD testing) is added for radical cure to eradicate hypnozoites.","why_wrong":{"0":"Persistence of blood stages causes recrudescence, which is typical of P. falciparum rather than vivax relapse.","1":"Gametocytes are the sexual forms infective to mosquitoes and do not cause clinical attacks in humans.","2":"Sporozoites leave the blood within about an hour to enter hepatocytes and do not persist for months.","3":"Sequestration of mature forms in deep capillaries is a feature of P. falciparum."}},{"id":27,"subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Parasitology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A barefoot farmer from rural Punjab has fatigue, pallor and microcytic hypochromic anaemia. He recalls an itchy rash on his feet some weeks earlier. Stool microscopy shows oval, thin-shelled eggs containing a segmented ovum (4-8 blastomeres). Which parasite is most likely?","options":["Ascaris lumbricoides","Enterobius vermicularis","Ancylostoma duodenale","Giardia lamblia","Trichuris trichiura"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hookworm (Ancylostoma duodenale, Necator americanus) filariform larvae penetrate the skin of bare feet ('ground itch'), migrate through the lungs and attach to the small intestinal mucosa, where they suck blood, causing iron-deficiency anaemia. Their eggs are oval, thin-shelled and contain a segmented ovum at 4-8 cell stage.","why_wrong":{"0":"Ascaris eggs have a thick, mammillated (bumpy) outer coat and infection is by ingestion, not through the skin.","1":"Enterobius eggs are planoconvex, found on perianal skin (tape test), and cause nocturnal perianal itching.","3":"Giardia is a protozoan causing malabsorption and steatorrhoea; it produces cysts, not helminth eggs.","4":"Trichuris eggs are barrel-shaped with bipolar plugs and infection follows ingestion of eggs."}},{"id":28,"subject":"Behavioral Sciences","topic":"Introduction to Behavioral Sciences","domain":"knowledge","question":"A doctor managing a patient with poorly controlled diabetes considers his insulin dosing, his low mood since losing his job, and his family's inability to afford healthy food. This approach to health and illness, which replaced the purely biomedical model, was proposed by:","options":["Sigmund Freud","George Engel","Abraham Maslow","Hans Selye","Elisabeth Kubler-Ross"],"answer":1,"explanation":"George Engel proposed the bio-psycho-social model in 1977, arguing that illness results from the interaction of biological, psychological and social factors and that all three must be addressed in assessment and management. It underpins holistic, patient-centred care taught in behavioural sciences.","why_wrong":{"0":"Freud founded psychoanalysis and the structural model of id, ego and superego.","2":"Maslow described the hierarchy of needs, from physiological needs to self-actualization.","3":"Selye described the general adaptation syndrome (alarm, resistance, exhaustion) in stress.","4":"Kubler-Ross described the five stages of grief in dying patients."}},{"id":29,"subject":"Behavioral Sciences","topic":"Understanding Behavior","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 70-year-old man admitted to a dimly lit ward at night is frightened because he sees a man standing behind the door; on switching on the light, it turns out to be a coat hanging on a hook. This experience is best described as a/an:","options":["Hallucination","Delusion","Depersonalization","Illusion","Derealization"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An illusion is a misinterpretation of a real external stimulus, common in dim light, fatigue, high emotion (affect illusion) and delirium. A hallucination, in contrast, is a perception in the absence of an external stimulus that is experienced as real.","why_wrong":{"0":"A hallucination is a perception without any external stimulus, whereas here a real coat was present.","1":"A delusion is a fixed false belief, a disorder of thought content rather than perception.","2":"Depersonalization is a feeling that oneself is unreal or detached from one's body.","4":"Derealization is a feeling that the surroundings are unreal or changed."}},{"id":30,"subject":"Behavioral Sciences","topic":"Stress and Stressors","domain":"knowledge","question":"A house officer is harshly reprimanded by his consultant in front of the whole team. He says nothing at work, but that evening shouts angrily at his younger brother over a trivial matter. Which defence mechanism is he using?","options":["Projection","Rationalization","Sublimation","Displacement","Reaction formation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Displacement is the redirection of emotions (here anger at the consultant) from the original, threatening source to a safer, less powerful target. It is an immature-to-neurotic defence mechanism in which the feeling is retained but its object is changed.","why_wrong":{"0":"Projection is attributing one's own unacceptable feelings or impulses to someone else.","1":"Rationalization is giving a plausible but false logical explanation for one's behaviour.","2":"Sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially acceptable, constructive activity.","4":"Reaction formation is behaving in a way opposite to one's unacceptable feelings."}},{"id":31,"subject":"Behavioral Sciences","topic":"Doctor-Patient Relationship","domain":"knowledge","question":"A young doctor notices that he becomes unusually irritated and dismissive with an elderly, demanding male patient who reminds him of his own strict father. This phenomenon is best termed:","options":["Countertransference","Transference","Projection","Identification","Splitting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Countertransference refers to the doctor's unconscious emotional reactions toward a patient, derived from the doctor's own past relationships. Recognizing it is important to maintain professional boundaries and objective clinical judgement; transference is the same process in the reverse direction (patient to doctor).","why_wrong":{"1":"Transference is the patient unconsciously redirecting feelings from past relationships onto the doctor.","2":"Projection is attributing one's own unacceptable impulses to another person, not re-experiencing past relationships.","3":"Identification is unconsciously adopting the characteristics of another person.","4":"Splitting is viewing people as all good or all bad, typical of borderline personality disorder."}},{"id":32,"subject":"Behavioral Sciences","topic":"Psychological Reactions","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 55-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer tells her doctor, \"If Allah only lets me live until my daughter's wedding next spring, I will accept whatever happens after that.\" According to Kubler-Ross, which stage of grief is she in?","options":["Denial","Anger","Bargaining","Depression","Acceptance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Kubler-Ross described five stages in response to impending death or loss: denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance; they need not occur in strict order. Bargaining involves attempts to negotiate, often with God or fate, for more time or a delay in the inevitable.","why_wrong":{"0":"In denial the patient refuses to believe the diagnosis, e.g. insisting the reports are wrong.","1":"In anger the patient asks 'why me?' and may be hostile toward family or staff.","3":"In depression the patient is withdrawn, tearful and preoccupied with losses.","4":"In acceptance the patient is calm and at peace, no longer striking deals for more time."}},{"id":33,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Neurophysiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 42-year-old man presents with progressive involuntary, jerky, dance-like movements of his limbs, irritability and declining memory. His father had a similar illness and died at 55 years. MRI shows atrophy of the heads of the caudate nuclei. Loss of which neurons is primarily responsible for his movement disorder?","options":["GABAergic medium spiny neurons of the striatum","Dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta","Glutamatergic neurons of the subthalamic nucleus","Cholinergic neurons of the nucleus basalis of Meynert","Purkinje cells of the cerebellar cortex"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Huntington disease (autosomal dominant CAG repeat expansion) causes early loss of GABA- (and enkephalin-) secreting medium spiny neurons in the caudate and putamen. Loss of striatal inhibition of the indirect pathway disinhibits the thalamocortical circuit, producing chorea; cortical neuron loss causes dementia.","why_wrong":{"1":"Loss of nigral dopaminergic neurons causes Parkinsonism (bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor), not chorea.","2":"Subthalamic nucleus lesions cause contralateral hemiballismus, not familial chorea with caudate atrophy.","3":"Loss of cholinergic neurons of the nucleus basalis of Meynert is characteristic of Alzheimer disease.","4":"Purkinje cell loss produces cerebellar ataxia, intention tremor and dysmetria, not chorea."}},{"id":34,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Neurophysiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A sensory receptor lies in series with skeletal muscle fibres at the musculotendinous junction, is stimulated by increased muscle tension, and on strong stimulation causes sudden relaxation of the same muscle (lengthening reaction). Which receptor and afferent fibre type are being described?","options":["Muscle spindle primary ending with type Ia afferent","Golgi tendon organ with type Ib afferent","Muscle spindle secondary ending with type II afferent","Pacinian corpuscle with A-beta afferent","Free nerve ending with type C afferent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Golgi tendon organ lies in series with extrafusal fibres and detects tension; its Ib afferents excite inhibitory interneurons in the cord, inhibiting the alpha motor neurons of the same muscle (autogenic inhibition / inverse stretch reflex). This protective lengthening reaction is the basis of clasp-knife rigidity.","why_wrong":{"0":"Spindle primary (Ia) endings lie in parallel with extrafusal fibres, detect length and rate of change, and excite the same muscle (stretch reflex).","2":"Spindle secondary (type II) endings signal static muscle length and do not cause autogenic inhibition.","3":"Pacinian corpuscles are rapidly adapting vibration/pressure receptors, not tension detectors in tendons.","4":"Free nerve endings with C fibres mediate slow pain, temperature and itch."}},{"id":35,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Neurophysiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 7-year-old boy is brought because of episodes in which he sits up screaming and appears terrified about 1-2 hours after falling asleep, and occasionally walks around the house; he has no recollection of these episodes in the morning. During which stage of sleep do these episodes most likely occur?","options":["REM sleep","NREM stage 1 (N1)","NREM stage 2 (N2)","NREM stage 3 (N3, slow-wave sleep)","Relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Night terrors and sleepwalking (parasomnias of arousal) arise from slow-wave (N3, delta-wave) sleep, which predominates in the first third of the night; the child is amnesic for the event. Nightmares, by contrast, occur in REM sleep, are recalled vividly, and cluster in the later part of the night.","why_wrong":{"0":"REM sleep is associated with nightmares and muscle atonia, so sleepwalking cannot occur in normal REM.","1":"N1 is light transition sleep with theta waves and hypnic jerks, not night terrors.","2":"N2 is characterised by sleep spindles and K-complexes; it is not the stage of arousal parasomnias.","4":"Relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed shows an alpha rhythm and is not a sleep stage."}},{"id":36,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Excitable Tissue (Nerve & Muscle)","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 60-year-old man with chronic kidney disease presents with generalised muscle weakness. Serum potassium is 7.6 mmol/L. What is the main mechanism by which severe hyperkalaemia reduces the excitability of his nerve and muscle cells?","options":["Hyperpolarisation of the resting membrane potential away from threshold","Sustained depolarisation of the resting membrane causing inactivation of voltage-gated Na+ channels","Competitive blockade of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the motor end-plate","Reduced Ca2+ entry into the presynaptic terminal decreasing acetylcholine release","Increased opening of chloride channels clamping the membrane at the chloride equilibrium potential"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Raising extracellular K+ reduces the K+ concentration gradient, so the resting membrane potential becomes less negative (depolarised). A modest rise increases excitability, but sustained depolarisation closes the inactivation gates of voltage-gated Na+ channels, so fewer channels are available and action potentials fail, causing weakness and paralysis.","why_wrong":{"0":"Hyperpolarisation is a feature of hypokalaemia, not hyperkalaemia.","2":"Nicotinic receptor blockade is the mechanism of non-depolarising neuromuscular blockers, not potassium.","3":"Reduced presynaptic Ca2+ entry is seen with hypermagnesaemia or Lambert-Eaton syndrome.","4":"Potassium does not act by opening chloride channels; that is the mechanism of GABA-A/glycine inhibition."}},{"id":37,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Excitable Tissue (Nerve & Muscle)","domain":"knowledge","question":"Which property distinguishes the NMDA-type glutamate receptor from the AMPA-type glutamate receptor?","options":["It is a metabotropic receptor coupled to a G protein","It is permeable only to Na+ and not to Ca2+","Its channel is blocked by Mg2+ at resting membrane potential","It is activated by GABA and conducts Cl- ions","It is blocked by tetrodotoxin"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The NMDA receptor is a ligand- and voltage-dependent ionotropic channel: it needs glutamate plus the co-agonist glycine, and its pore is plugged by Mg2+ until the membrane is depolarised (usually via AMPA receptors). It is highly permeable to Ca2+, which underlies long-term potentiation and excitotoxicity.","why_wrong":{"0":"NMDA receptors are ionotropic ligand-gated channels; metabotropic glutamate receptors are the G-protein-coupled type.","1":"NMDA receptors are notably permeable to Ca2+ as well as Na+ and K+.","3":"GABA-A receptors, not glutamate receptors, are activated by GABA and conduct Cl-.","4":"Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels, not ligand-gated glutamate receptors."}},{"id":38,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Cardiovascular Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 58-year-old man with chronic stable angina and a resting heart rate of 92/min, in whom beta-blockers are contraindicated, is started on ivabradine. His heart rate falls to 70/min without any change in myocardial contractility. Ivabradine slows the heart by inhibiting which current in the sinoatrial node?","options":["L-type Ca2+ current responsible for phase 0","Funny current (If) through HCN channels during phase 4","Delayed rectifier K+ current responsible for phase 3","Fast voltage-gated Na+ current","Inward rectifier K+ current (IK1) maintaining phase 4 at a stable level"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation in the SA node is initiated by the 'funny' current (If), an inward Na+-dominated current through HCN channels that opens on hyperpolarisation; T-type Ca2+ current completes the slope. Ivabradine selectively blocks If, flattening the phase 4 slope and reducing heart rate without negative inotropy.","why_wrong":{"0":"L-type Ca2+ channels produce the nodal upstroke (phase 0); blocking them (e.g. verapamil) also reduces contractility.","2":"Delayed rectifier K+ current repolarises the cell (phase 3); its blockade (class III drugs) prolongs repolarisation.","3":"Fast Na+ channels produce phase 0 in atrial/ventricular muscle and Purkinje fibres, not in the SA node.","4":"IK1 keeps the resting potential stable in ventricular muscle; it is sparse in nodal cells and not ivabradine's target."}},{"id":39,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Cardiovascular Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"The plateau phase (phase 2) of the ventricular myocyte action potential, during which inward L-type Ca2+ current balances outward K+ current, corresponds to which part of the surface ECG?","options":["P wave","PR segment","QRS complex","ST segment","T wave"],"answer":3,"explanation":"During the plateau all ventricular cells are uniformly depolarised, so no potential difference exists between regions and the ECG records an isoelectric ST segment. QRS reflects phase 0 depolarisation of the ventricles and the T wave reflects phase 3 repolarisation.","why_wrong":{"0":"The P wave represents atrial depolarisation.","1":"The PR segment represents conduction delay through the AV node and His-Purkinje system.","2":"The QRS complex corresponds to phase 0 (rapid depolarisation) of the ventricles.","4":"The T wave corresponds to phase 3 (rapid repolarisation) of the ventricles."}},{"id":40,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Cardiovascular Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 62-year-old man has a routine ECG. The QRS complex is predominantly positive in lead I and predominantly negative in leads II and aVF. QRS duration is 100 ms. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this finding?","options":["Left anterior fascicular block","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Left posterior fascicular block","Cor pulmonale due to chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Tall, thin body habitus"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Positive lead I with negative lead II and aVF places the mean QRS axis beyond -30 degrees, i.e. left axis deviation. Left anterior fascicular block is a classic cause (others: inferior MI, left ventricular hypertrophy). A normal QRS duration fits a fascicular rather than a bundle branch block.","why_wrong":{"1":"Right ventricular hypertrophy shifts the axis to the right (negative lead I, positive aVF).","2":"Left posterior fascicular block produces right axis deviation.","3":"Cor pulmonale causes right ventricular strain and right axis deviation.","4":"A tall, thin habitus with a vertical heart tends towards a rightward (vertical) axis."}},{"id":41,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Respiratory Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 58-year-old man has progressive exertional dyspnoea and fine bibasal 'velcro' crackles. High-resolution CT shows subpleural honeycombing consistent with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. Which pattern of pulmonary function tests is expected?","options":["FEV1/FVC reduced, TLC increased, DLCO reduced","FEV1/FVC normal or increased, TLC reduced, DLCO reduced","FEV1/FVC normal or increased, TLC reduced, DLCO normal","FEV1/FVC reduced, TLC normal, DLCO normal","FEV1/FVC normal, TLC increased, DLCO increased"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pulmonary fibrosis is a parenchymal restrictive disease: stiff lungs reduce all volumes (TLC, FVC), FEV1 falls proportionately or less (increased radial traction), so FEV1/FVC is normal or high. Thickening of the alveolar-capillary membrane and loss of capillary bed reduce DLCO.","why_wrong":{"0":"Reduced ratio with hyperinflation and low DLCO is the pattern of emphysema.","2":"Restriction with normal DLCO suggests extraparenchymal causes such as obesity, kyphoscoliosis or neuromuscular weakness.","3":"Reduced ratio with normal DLCO suggests chronic bronchitis or asthma (obstruction without alveolar destruction).","4":"Increased TLC and DLCO with a normal ratio does not match fibrosis; DLCO may be raised in polycythaemia or alveolar haemorrhage."}},{"id":42,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Respiratory Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A family is brought to the emergency department after spending the night in a closed room heated by a gas heater. They complain of headache and confusion; pulse oximetry reads 98%. In carbon monoxide poisoning, which set of changes is expected?","options":["PaO2 normal, arterial O2 content reduced, O2-Hb dissociation curve shifted to the left","PaO2 reduced, arterial O2 content reduced, O2-Hb dissociation curve shifted to the right","PaO2 normal, arterial O2 content normal, O2-Hb dissociation curve shifted to the right","PaO2 reduced, arterial O2 content normal, O2-Hb dissociation curve shifted to the left","PaO2 normal, arterial O2 content reduced, O2-Hb dissociation curve shifted to the right"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CO binds Hb with ~200-250 times the affinity of O2, reducing the Hb available to carry O2 and hence O2 content, while dissolved O2 and PaO2 remain normal. CO also increases the O2 affinity of the remaining haem sites, shifting the curve to the left and impairing unloading in tissues. Standard pulse oximeters misread carboxyhaemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin, so co-oximetry is needed.","why_wrong":{"1":"Low PaO2 with right shift fits hypoxaemic hypoxia with acidosis, not CO poisoning (PaO2 is normal).","2":"O2 content is reduced in CO poisoning and the curve shifts left, not right.","3":"PaO2 is normal in CO poisoning because alveolar gas exchange is unaffected.","4":"CO shifts the curve to the left (increased affinity), not to the right."}},{"id":43,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Hematology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 2-year-old boy presents with a swollen, painful right knee after a minor fall. His maternal uncle had a similar bleeding problem. Platelet count and bleeding time are normal, PT is normal and aPTT is prolonged; the aPTT corrects on mixing with normal plasma. Deficiency of which factor is most likely?","options":["Factor VII","Factor VIII","von Willebrand factor","Factor XII","Factor XIII"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Haemarthrosis in a boy with an affected maternal uncle suggests X-linked haemophilia; the commonest form is haemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency). Isolated prolonged aPTT that corrects with mixing indicates an intrinsic pathway factor deficiency rather than an inhibitor, with normal PT and normal bleeding time (platelet function intact).","why_wrong":{"0":"Factor VII deficiency prolongs the PT (extrinsic pathway) with a normal aPTT.","2":"von Willebrand disease is autosomal, causes mucocutaneous bleeding and a prolonged bleeding time.","3":"Factor XII deficiency prolongs aPTT but does not cause clinical bleeding.","4":"Factor XIII deficiency gives normal PT and aPTT; diagnosis needs a clot solubility (urea) test."}},{"id":44,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Hematology","domain":"knowledge","question":"In adults, the majority (about 90%) of circulating erythropoietin is produced in response to tissue hypoxia by which cells?","options":["Peritubular interstitial cells of the renal cortex","Juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole","Hepatocytes","Macula densa cells of the distal tubule","Glomerular mesangial cells"],"answer":0,"explanation":"About 90% of erythropoietin is produced by the kidneys, mainly by fibroblast-like peritubular interstitial cells in the cortex and outer medulla, under control of hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF). The liver contributes about 10% (the main source in the fetus), which is why chronic kidney disease causes a normocytic anaemia.","why_wrong":{"1":"Juxtaglomerular cells secrete renin, not erythropoietin.","2":"Hepatocytes produce only about 10% of erythropoietin in adults (main fetal source).","3":"Macula densa cells sense distal tubular NaCl for tubuloglomerular feedback.","4":"Mesangial cells provide structural support and phagocytosis, and are not the main EPO source."}},{"id":45,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"At low concentrations, angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole of the glomerulus. Which combination of changes in glomerular filtration rate (GFR), renal plasma flow (RPF) and filtration fraction (FF) results?","options":["GFR increased, RPF decreased, FF increased","GFR decreased, RPF decreased, FF unchanged","GFR increased, RPF increased, FF unchanged","GFR decreased, RPF increased, FF decreased","GFR unchanged, RPF unchanged, FF decreased"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Efferent constriction raises glomerular hydrostatic pressure (maintaining or slightly increasing GFR) while increasing resistance to flow, so RPF falls. Since FF = GFR/RPF, filtration fraction rises. This is how angiotensin II preserves GFR in hypovolaemia, and why ACE inhibitors can drop GFR in renal artery stenosis.","why_wrong":{"1":"Fall in both GFR and RPF with unchanged FF is the effect of afferent arteriolar constriction.","2":"Rise in both GFR and RPF is the effect of afferent arteriolar dilatation (e.g. prostaglandins).","3":"Lower GFR with higher RPF is the effect of efferent arteriolar dilatation (e.g. ACE inhibitors).","4":"Efferent constriction does change GFR and RPF and increases, rather than decreases, FF."}},{"id":46,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A healthy volunteer is deprived of water for 12 hours. His plasma osmolality is 300 mOsm/kg H2O, urine osmolality is 600 mOsm/kg H2O and urine flow rate is 2 mL/min. What is his free-water clearance?","options":["+2 mL/min","-2 mL/min","+4 mL/min","-4 mL/min","0 mL/min"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Osmolar clearance = (Uosm x V)/Posm = (600 x 2)/300 = 4 mL/min. Free-water clearance = V - Cosm = 2 - 4 = -2 mL/min. A negative value means solute-free water is being reabsorbed (concentrated urine under ADH), as expected during water deprivation.","why_wrong":{"0":"+2 mL/min would mean dilute urine (free water excretion), impossible when urine is hyperosmotic to plasma.","2":"4 mL/min is the osmolar clearance, not the free-water clearance.","3":"-4 mL/min wrongly takes the negative of the osmolar clearance and ignores the urine flow term (CH2O = V - Cosm).","4":"Zero free-water clearance occurs only when urine is isosmotic with plasma."}},{"id":47,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 19-year-old man with type 1 diabetes presents with vomiting and rapid deep breathing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.27, PaCO2 25 mmHg, HCO3- 11 mmol/L. Which of the following best describes his acid-base status?","options":["Metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","Metabolic acidosis with a superimposed respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis with a superimposed respiratory alkalosis","Primary respiratory alkalosis with metabolic compensation","Primary respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Low pH with low HCO3- indicates a primary metabolic acidosis (diabetic ketoacidosis). Winter's formula predicts PaCO2 = 1.5 x HCO3- + 8 +/- 2 = 1.5 x 11 + 8 = 24.5 (range 22.5-26.5 mmHg); the measured 25 mmHg lies within it, so respiratory compensation (Kussmaul breathing) is appropriate.","why_wrong":{"1":"A concomitant respiratory acidosis would give a PaCO2 above the predicted range (above about 26.5 mmHg).","2":"A concomitant respiratory alkalosis would give a PaCO2 below the predicted range (below about 22.5 mmHg).","3":"Primary respiratory alkalosis would produce an alkalaemic or near-normal pH, not a pH of 7.27.","4":"Respiratory acidosis requires a raised PaCO2; here PaCO2 is low."}},{"id":48,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Endocrine Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 4-year-old girl is brought with clitoral enlargement, pubic hair and accelerated growth. Her blood pressure is 130/90 mmHg and serum potassium is 3.0 mmol/L. Plasma 11-deoxycortisol is markedly raised. Deficiency of which enzyme is most likely?","options":["21-hydroxylase","11-beta-hydroxylase","17-alpha-hydroxylase","3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","Aldosterone synthase (18-hydroxylase)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency, cortisol synthesis is blocked, ACTH rises and precursors are shunted to adrenal androgens (virilisation). Accumulated 11-deoxycorticosterone has mineralocorticoid activity, causing hypertension and hypokalaemia despite low renin and aldosterone; raised 11-deoxycortisol is the marker.","why_wrong":{"0":"21-hydroxylase deficiency causes virilisation with salt wasting, hypotension and hyperkalaemia; 17-hydroxyprogesterone is raised.","2":"17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency causes hypertension and hypokalaemia but no androgen excess (sexual infantilism in girls).","3":"3-beta-HSD deficiency causes salt wasting and only mild virilisation of girls, without hypertension.","4":"Aldosterone synthase deficiency causes isolated salt wasting and hyperkalaemia without virilisation."}},{"id":49,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Endocrine Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 16-year-old girl with diabetic ketoacidosis has a serum potassium of 5.3 mmol/L at presentation. Two hours after starting an intravenous insulin infusion, her serum potassium falls to 3.1 mmol/L. By which direct action does insulin lower serum potassium?","options":["Stimulation of aldosterone secretion increasing renal K+ excretion","Stimulation of Na+-K+-ATPase shifting K+ into skeletal muscle and liver cells","Inhibition of H+-K+-ATPase in the intercalated cells of the collecting duct","Induction of osmotic diuresis increasing urinary K+ loss","Increased K+ secretion by colonic epithelium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Insulin stimulates Na+-K+-ATPase activity, mainly in skeletal muscle and liver, driving K+ into cells; this internal shift is rapid and is the basis of insulin-dextrose therapy for hyperkalaemia. Patients in DKA have a total-body K+ deficit despite normal or high serum K+, so potassium must be replaced once insulin is started.","why_wrong":{"0":"Insulin does not act by stimulating aldosterone, and renal excretion is too slow to explain this rapid fall.","2":"Inhibiting H+-K+-ATPase would reduce K+ reabsorption only marginally and is not insulin's mechanism.","3":"Osmotic diuresis is caused by hyperglycaemia and is reduced, not induced, by insulin.","4":"Colonic K+ secretion is regulated by aldosterone, not insulin, and is quantitatively minor."}},{"id":50,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Endocrine Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 46,XY infant with 5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency has normally functioning testes. Which of the following structures will develop normally?","options":["Penile urethra","Scrotum","Prostate","Epididymis","Glans penis"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Testosterone itself drives differentiation of the Wolffian duct into the epididymis, vas deferens and seminal vesicles, so these develop normally. Dihydrotestosterone (made by 5-alpha-reductase) is needed for the external genitalia (penis, scrotum, penile urethra) and the prostate, which are therefore underdeveloped or ambiguous at birth; virilisation often occurs at puberty.","why_wrong":{"0":"Formation of the penile urethra by fusion of the urethral folds requires DHT.","1":"Fusion of the labioscrotal swellings into a scrotum requires DHT.","2":"The prostate develops from the urogenital sinus under the influence of DHT.","4":"The glans penis develops from the genital tubercle, which requires DHT."}},{"id":51,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"Gastrointestinal Physiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"During fasting, bursts of intense peristaltic contractions sweep from the stomach to the terminal ileum about every 90 minutes, clearing undigested residue and bacteria. These are abolished by feeding. Which hormone mediates this pattern, and is mimicked by low-dose erythromycin?","options":["Gastrin","Cholecystokinin","Secretin","Motilin","Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The migrating motor complex (MMC) is the interdigestive 'housekeeper' pattern, occurring every 90-120 minutes in the fasting state. Phase III contractions are initiated by motilin released from duodenal M cells; erythromycin is a motilin-receptor agonist used as a prokinetic in gastroparesis.","why_wrong":{"0":"Gastrin stimulates gastric acid secretion and mucosal growth; it is released after meals.","1":"CCK stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion and slows gastric emptying.","2":"Secretin stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate secretion in response to duodenal acid.","4":"GIP stimulates insulin release after oral glucose (incretin effect)."}},{"id":52,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Genetics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 9-year-old boy has had severe sunburn after minimal sun exposure since infancy. He now has extensive freckling, dry atrophic skin and two basal cell carcinomas on his face. His cultured fibroblasts cannot remove thymine dimers after UV irradiation. Which DNA repair mechanism is defective?","options":["Nucleotide excision repair","Mismatch repair","Base excision repair","Non-homologous end joining","Homologous recombination repair"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Xeroderma pigmentosum (autosomal recessive) results from defects in nucleotide excision repair, which uses UV-specific endonucleases to excise a short oligonucleotide containing pyrimidine (thymine) dimers. Patients develop photosensitivity and skin cancers (BCC, SCC, melanoma) at a very young age.","why_wrong":{"1":"Defective mismatch repair (MSH2, MLH1) causes Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) with microsatellite instability.","2":"Base excision repair removes damaged single bases (e.g. deaminated cytosine) via glycosylases, not thymine dimers.","3":"Non-homologous end joining repairs double-strand breaks; defects cause immunodeficiency and radiosensitivity.","4":"Homologous recombination repairs double-strand breaks; defects (BRCA1/2) cause breast and ovarian cancer."}},{"id":53,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Genetics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A researcher wants to determine whether a particular gene is being transcribed in human hepatocytes by detecting and sizing its messenger RNA in a tissue extract. Which technique is most appropriate?","options":["Southern blot","Northern blot","Western blot","Southwestern blot","Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Northern blotting separates RNA by electrophoresis, transfers it to a membrane and hybridises it with a labelled complementary probe, detecting a specific mRNA and its size. Mnemonic SNoW DRoP: Southern = DNA, Northern = RNA, Western = Protein.","why_wrong":{"0":"Southern blot detects specific DNA sequences, which are present whether or not the gene is transcribed.","2":"Western blot detects proteins using antibodies, not mRNA.","3":"Southwestern blot detects DNA-binding proteins (e.g. transcription factors).","4":"ELISA detects antigens or antibodies, not specific mRNA sequences."}},{"id":54,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Genetics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A family is admitted with vomiting and watery diarrhoea starting 10 hours after eating wild mushrooms. On day 3 they develop jaundice, rising transaminases and coagulopathy. The toxin responsible (alpha-amanitin) inhibits which enzyme?","options":["RNA polymerase I","RNA polymerase II","RNA polymerase III","DNA polymerase delta","Peptidyl transferase of the 60S ribosomal subunit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Alpha-amanitin from Amanita phalloides strongly inhibits eukaryotic RNA polymerase II, blocking synthesis of mRNA (and most snRNAs); hepatocytes are most affected, causing fulminant hepatic failure. RNA pol III is only inhibited at high concentrations and RNA pol I is resistant.","why_wrong":{"0":"RNA polymerase I synthesises 45S pre-rRNA in the nucleolus and is insensitive to alpha-amanitin.","2":"RNA polymerase III (tRNA, 5S rRNA) is only inhibited at high concentrations of alpha-amanitin.","3":"DNA polymerase delta is involved in DNA replication (lagging strand), not targeted by alpha-amanitin.","4":"Peptidyl transferase on the 60S subunit is inhibited by cycloheximide, not alpha-amanitin."}},{"id":55,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Amino Acids and Proteins","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 3-day-old boy develops poor feeding, vomiting, lethargy and tachypnoea after starting breastfeeding. Plasma ammonia is markedly raised, plasma citrulline is low and urinary orotic acid is increased. Blood counts are normal. Deficiency of which enzyme is most likely?","options":["Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I","Ornithine transcarbamylase","Argininosuccinate synthetase","Arginase","UMP synthase"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency is the commonest urea cycle disorder and is X-linked. Accumulated carbamoyl phosphate leaks into the cytosol and enters pyrimidine synthesis, raising orotic acid; citrulline is low because it cannot be formed. Hyperammonaemia causes encephalopathy and respiratory alkalosis.","why_wrong":{"0":"CPS-I deficiency causes hyperammonaemia with low citrulline but no orotic aciduria, since carbamoyl phosphate is not formed.","2":"Argininosuccinate synthetase deficiency (citrullinaemia) causes markedly raised, not low, citrulline.","3":"Arginase deficiency causes raised arginine and progressive spastic diplegia, usually with mild hyperammonaemia.","4":"UMP synthase deficiency (hereditary orotic aciduria) causes megaloblastic anaemia without hyperammonaemia."}},{"id":56,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Enzymes","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 55-year-old man is admitted with an acute ST-elevation myocardial infarction. On day 4 of admission he develops fresh chest pain. Which serum marker is most useful to detect a re-infarction at this stage?","options":["Troponin I","CK-MB","LDH-1","Aspartate aminotransferase","CK-MM"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CK-MB rises 4-6 hours after infarction, peaks at about 24 hours and returns to normal within 48-72 hours, so a fresh rise on day 4 indicates re-infarction. Troponins remain elevated for 7-10 days (troponin T up to 14 days) after the original infarct, making a new event hard to identify. Note: current guidelines (4th Universal Definition of MI) also diagnose re-infarction by a >20% rise in serial high-sensitivity troponin; CK-MB remains the classic textbook answer.","why_wrong":{"0":"Troponin I stays raised for 7-10 days after the first infarct, so a single value cannot separate a new event (only a further >20% rise on serial testing can).","2":"LDH-1 rises late (24-48 h) and stays raised for 10-14 days; it was historically used for late presentation.","3":"AST is non-specific (liver, muscle) and is no longer used for diagnosing myocardial infarction.","4":"CK-MM is the skeletal muscle isoenzyme and is not specific for myocardial injury."}},{"id":57,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Vitamins and Minerals","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 50-year-old woman with a metastatic ileal carcinoid tumour has episodic flushing and diarrhoea. Over several months she develops a scaly, hyperpigmented rash on sun-exposed skin of her neck and forearms and becomes forgetful. Deficiency of which vitamin best explains her new features?","options":["Thiamine","Riboflavin","Niacin","Pyridoxine","Cobalamin"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Carcinoid tumours divert up to 60% of tryptophan to serotonin synthesis, leaving little for endogenous niacin (nicotinic acid) synthesis. The result is pellagra: photosensitive dermatitis (Casal necklace), diarrhoea and dementia. Hartnup disease and isoniazid therapy are other causes.","why_wrong":{"0":"Thiamine deficiency causes beriberi and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, not a photosensitive rash.","1":"Riboflavin deficiency causes angular stomatitis, cheilosis and magenta tongue.","3":"Pyridoxine deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy, sideroblastic anaemia and convulsions; it is a cofactor in niacin synthesis but the carcinoid mechanism is tryptophan diversion.","4":"Cobalamin deficiency causes megaloblastic anaemia and subacute combined degeneration of the cord."}},{"id":58,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Vitamins and Minerals","domain":"knowledge","question":"In anaemia of chronic disease, raised hepcidin reduces dietary iron absorption and traps iron in macrophages. Hepcidin produces these effects by binding to and causing degradation of which protein?","options":["Divalent metal transporter 1 (DMT1)","Ferroportin","Transferrin receptor 1","Duodenal cytochrome b (ferric reductase)","Ferritin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hepcidin, made by hepatocytes in response to iron overload and inflammation (IL-6), binds ferroportin, the only known cellular iron exporter on the basolateral enterocyte membrane and on macrophages, causing its internalisation and degradation. Iron is therefore retained in enterocytes and macrophages, lowering serum iron while ferritin stays high.","why_wrong":{"0":"DMT1 transports Fe2+ across the apical enterocyte membrane and is not the direct target of hepcidin.","2":"Transferrin receptor 1 mediates cellular uptake of transferrin-bound iron, not export.","3":"Duodenal cytochrome b reduces dietary Fe3+ to Fe2+ at the brush border.","4":"Ferritin is the intracellular iron storage protein, not the target of hepcidin."}},{"id":59,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 12-year-old girl has short stature, a round face, obesity and short fourth and fifth metacarpals. Investigations show low serum calcium, high phosphate and markedly raised PTH; infusion of PTH fails to increase urinary cAMP. What is the most likely molecular defect?","options":["Inactivating mutation of the Gs-alpha subunit (GNAS)","Activating mutation of the Gs-alpha subunit (GNAS)","Inactivating mutation of the Gi-alpha subunit","Activating mutation of the calcium-sensing receptor","Deletion of the PTH gene"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a with Albright hereditary osteodystrophy: loss-of-function of Gs-alpha prevents the PTH receptor from activating adenylyl cyclase, so there is end-organ resistance with high PTH and no rise in urinary cAMP. Resistance to other Gs-coupled hormones (TSH, gonadotrophins) may also occur.","why_wrong":{"1":"Activating Gs-alpha mutations (somatic mosaic) cause McCune-Albright syndrome with precocious puberty and fibrous dysplasia.","2":"Gi-alpha is the target of pertussis toxin and not the cause of PTH resistance.","3":"Activating calcium-sensing receptor mutations cause hypocalcaemia with low or inappropriately normal PTH.","4":"Absent PTH (true hypoparathyroidism) gives low PTH and a normal cAMP response to infused PTH."}},{"id":60,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Energy Metabolism","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 24-year-old bodybuilder is brought to the emergency department with temperature 41 C, profuse sweating, tachycardia and tachypnoea after taking an unlicensed 'fat-burning' product containing 2,4-dinitrophenol. Which mechanism explains his hyperthermia?","options":["Inhibition of cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV)","Inhibition of the F0 component of ATP synthase","Dissipation of the inner mitochondrial proton gradient","Inhibition of NADH dehydrogenase (complex I)","Inhibition of the ADP/ATP translocase"],"answer":2,"explanation":"2,4-Dinitrophenol is a lipid-soluble proton carrier (uncoupler) that dissipates the proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane. Electron transport and O2 consumption increase while ATP synthesis falls, and the energy is released as heat. Thermogenin (UCP-1) in brown fat works similarly.","why_wrong":{"0":"Complex IV inhibition (cyanide, CO, azide) stops electron transport and O2 consumption, causing hypoxic-like toxicity rather than heat production.","1":"Oligomycin inhibits the F0 portion of ATP synthase, which reduces (not increases) O2 consumption in coupled mitochondria.","3":"Complex I inhibition (rotenone, amytal) reduces electron flow and O2 consumption.","4":"Atractyloside inhibits the ADP/ATP translocase, reducing ATP synthesis and O2 consumption."}},{"id":61,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Energy Metabolism","domain":"knowledge","question":"An 18-month-old boy is brought unconscious after a prolonged fast during a febrile illness with vomiting. Blood glucose is 1.8 mmol/L and urine is negative for ketones. Liver is mildly enlarged. Plasma acylcarnitine profile shows raised octanoylcarnitine (C8), and urine contains dicarboxylic acids. Deficiency of which enzyme is most likely?","options":["Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase","Glucose-6-phosphatase","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase","HMG-CoA reductase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"MCAD deficiency, the commonest fatty acid oxidation defect, impairs beta-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids during fasting, so the liver cannot produce acetyl-CoA for ketogenesis or support gluconeogenesis, causing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia. Medium-chain acylcarnitines (C8) accumulate and omega-oxidation produces dicarboxylic aciduria. Treatment is avoiding fasting.","why_wrong":{"1":"Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (von Gierke) causes fasting hypoglycaemia with lactic acidosis, hyperuricaemia and marked hepatomegaly, without raised C8 acylcarnitine.","2":"Muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency (McArdle) causes exercise-induced cramps and myoglobinuria, not hypoglycaemia.","3":"Propionyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency causes propionic acidaemia with metabolic acidosis, ketosis and hyperammonaemia.","4":"HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis; its deficiency does not cause this picture."}},{"id":62,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Energy Metabolism","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 10-year-old boy has had recurrent episodes of acute pancreatitis. Examination shows eruptive xanthomas on his buttocks and lipaemia retinalis. A fasting plasma sample left overnight in the refrigerator shows a creamy top layer over a clear infranatant. Serum triglycerides are 2,500 mg/dL; apolipoprotein C-II level is normal. Which protein is most likely deficient?","options":["LDL receptor","Apolipoprotein E","Lipoprotein lipase","Hormone-sensitive lipase","Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A creamy layer over a clear infranatant indicates accumulation of chylomicrons (type I hyperlipoproteinaemia, familial chylomicronaemia syndrome). It is caused by deficiency of lipoprotein lipase or its activator apo C-II; with normal apo C-II, LPL deficiency is the cause. Very high triglycerides cause pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas and lipaemia retinalis.","why_wrong":{"0":"LDL receptor defects (familial hypercholesterolaemia) cause raised LDL cholesterol, tendon xanthomas and premature coronary disease.","1":"Apo E2 homozygosity causes dysbetalipoproteinaemia (type III) with remnant accumulation and palmar xanthomas.","3":"Hormone-sensitive lipase mobilises triglycerides from adipose stores and is not responsible for chylomicron clearance.","4":"MTP deficiency causes abetalipoproteinaemia with absent chylomicrons and very low triglycerides."}},{"id":63,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Cell Injury","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 52-year-old man who has smoked 30 pack-years undergoes bronchoscopy for chronic cough. Biopsy of the main bronchus shows the normal pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium replaced by mature stratified squamous epithelium without nuclear atypia. Which cellular adaptation is this?","options":["Hyperplasia","Dysplasia","Metaplasia","Hypertrophy","Anaplasia"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Metaplasia is a reversible change in which one adult differentiated cell type is replaced by another better able to withstand stress; in smokers ciliated columnar epithelium is replaced by squamous epithelium (squamous metaplasia). It results from reprogramming of stem cells, not transformation of mature cells. Persistent metaplasia may progress to dysplasia and squamous cell carcinoma, and protective functions such as mucus secretion and ciliary clearance are lost.","why_wrong":{"0":"Hyperplasia is an increase in the number of cells of the same type, not replacement by a different epithelium.","1":"Dysplasia implies disordered growth with cytological atypia, which is absent here.","3":"Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size, not a change in cell type.","4":"Anaplasia is lack of differentiation seen in malignant tumours, not mature squamous epithelium."}},{"id":64,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Cell Injury","domain":"knowledge","question":"On electron microscopy of myocardium from an ischaemic area, which of the following findings indicates that cell injury has become irreversible?","options":["Large flocculent amorphous densities in mitochondria","Generalised cellular swelling","Plasma membrane blebbing","Detachment of ribosomes from the rough endoplasmic reticulum","Clumping of nuclear chromatin"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Irreversible injury is characterised by the inability to restore mitochondrial function and profound membrane damage. Large flocculent amorphous densities in mitochondria (in ischaemic myocardium), rupture of lysosomes and plasma membrane, and nuclear changes (pyknosis, karyorrhexis, karyolysis) mark the point of no return. Swelling, blebbing, ribosome detachment and chromatin clumping can all reverse if the insult is removed.","why_wrong":{"1":"Cellular swelling from failure of the Na+/K+ ATPase is the earliest feature of reversible injury.","2":"Blebbing and loss of microvilli are features of reversible injury.","3":"Ribosomal detachment and dilated ER are reversible ultrastructural changes.","4":"Mild chromatin clumping is reversible; nuclear dissolution (karyolysis, pyknosis, karyorrhexis) marks irreversibility."}},{"id":65,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Inflammation and Repair","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 40-year-old man with right lower lobe pneumonia develops a pleural effusion. Aspirated fluid is turbid, has a specific gravity of 1.025, pleural fluid/serum protein ratio of 0.7 and pleural fluid/serum LDH ratio of 0.8, with many neutrophils. What is the principal mechanism of fluid accumulation?","options":["Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Reduced plasma oncotic pressure","Renal retention of sodium and water","Increased vascular permeability","Transient arteriolar vasoconstriction"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The fluid meets Light's criteria for an exudate (pleural/serum protein ratio >0.5, pleural/serum LDH ratio >0.6; the third criterion is pleural LDH >2/3 of the upper limit of normal serum LDH), and its specific gravity >1.020 and many neutrophils also indicate an inflammatory exudate. Exudates form because inflammation increases vascular permeability, chiefly by endothelial cell contraction in post-capillary venules (histamine, bradykinin, leukotrienes) and direct endothelial injury, allowing protein-rich fluid to escape. Transudates result from altered hydrostatic or oncotic pressure with intact vessels.","why_wrong":{"0":"Raised hydrostatic pressure (e.g. heart failure) produces a protein-poor transudate.","1":"Hypoalbuminaemia (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis) produces a transudate.","2":"Sodium retention expands plasma volume and produces transudative oedema.","4":"Brief vasoconstriction is an early, inconsequential vascular change and does not cause fluid leakage."}},{"id":66,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Inflammation and Repair","domain":"knowledge","question":"Bacterial endotoxin stimulates leukocytes to release IL-1 and TNF, which induce cyclooxygenase in the hypothalamic vasculature. Which arachidonic acid metabolite then acts on the hypothalamic thermoregulatory centre to raise the set point and produce fever?","options":["Prostacyclin (PGI2)","Prostaglandin E2","Thromboxane A2","Leukotriene B4","Leukotriene C4"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Exogenous pyrogens (e.g. LPS) stimulate release of endogenous pyrogens IL-1 and TNF, which increase cyclooxygenase activity and conversion of arachidonic acid to PGE2 in the hypothalamus. PGE2 raises cAMP and resets the thermoregulatory set point upwards. NSAIDs (aspirin) and paracetamol reduce fever by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis.","why_wrong":{"0":"PGI2 is produced by endothelium and causes vasodilation and inhibits platelet aggregation.","2":"TXA2 from platelets causes vasoconstriction and platelet aggregation.","3":"LTB4 is a potent neutrophil chemotactic agent, not the mediator of fever.","4":"LTC4 causes bronchospasm, vasoconstriction and increased permeability."}},{"id":67,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Wound Healing","domain":"knowledge","question":"A biopsy is taken from the edge of a sutured surgical wound on the 4th post-operative day. It shows granulation tissue with proliferating fibroblasts, new capillaries and early extracellular matrix. Which collagen type predominates in this early granulation tissue before being replaced in the maturing scar?","options":["Type I collagen","Type II collagen","Type IV collagen","Type VII collagen","Type III collagen"],"answer":4,"explanation":"Early granulation tissue and newly deposited matrix are rich in type III collagen, which is gradually replaced by the stronger type I collagen during scar remodelling. Wound strength is only about 10% of normal skin at the end of the first week and plateaus at about 70-80% by 3 months. Vitamin C deficiency impairs hydroxylation of collagen and delays healing.","why_wrong":{"0":"Type I collagen replaces type III as the scar matures and gives the final tensile strength.","1":"Type II collagen is found in hyaline cartilage and vitreous humour.","2":"Type IV collagen forms basement membranes.","3":"Type VII collagen forms anchoring fibrils at the dermo-epidermal junction."}},{"id":68,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Haemodynamics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 62-year-old woman with deep vein thrombosis after hip surgery develops pleuritic chest pain and haemoptysis. A CT scan shows a peripheral wedge-shaped lesion; at autopsy weeks later this is confirmed as a haemorrhagic (red) infarct. Which feature best explains why the infarct is haemorrhagic?","options":["End-arterial supply with no collateral circulation","Dual blood supply from bronchial and pulmonary arteries","Dense solid parenchyma limiting blood seepage","Superimposed bacterial infection of the infarct","Liquefactive necrosis typical of lung tissue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Red (haemorrhagic) infarcts occur with venous occlusion (e.g. testicular torsion), in loose tissues, in organs with dual circulation (lung, small intestine), in previously congested tissues and on reperfusion. In the lung, bronchial arterial blood continues to flow into the necrotic area supplied by the occluded pulmonary artery. White infarcts occur with arterial occlusion in solid organs with end-arterial supply such as heart, spleen and kidney.","why_wrong":{"0":"End-arterial supply (heart, spleen, kidney) produces pale white infarcts.","2":"Solid organs limit seepage of blood into the necrotic area and give white infarcts.","3":"Infection converts an infarct into a septic infarct or abscess, not specifically a red one.","4":"Lung infarcts show coagulative necrosis; liquefactive infarcts are characteristic of the brain."}},{"id":69,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Haemodynamics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 24-year-old man sustains a closed fracture of the shaft of the femur in a road traffic accident. Two days later he becomes breathless and confused, and petechiae appear over his chest, axillae and conjunctivae. Which type of embolism is most likely?","options":["Pulmonary thromboembolism","Air embolism","Amniotic fluid embolism","Fat embolism","Septic embolism"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Fat embolism syndrome typically develops 1-3 days after fractures of long bones (or soft tissue crush injury) as marrow fat globules enter the circulation. The classic triad is pulmonary insufficiency, neurological symptoms (confusion, coma) and a petechial rash due to thrombocytopenia and microvascular occlusion. Free fatty acids released from the globules cause additional endothelial injury.","why_wrong":{"0":"Thromboembolism causes dyspnoea and chest pain but not a petechial rash with confusion at 48 hours.","1":"Air embolism follows entry of air into veins (neck surgery, decompression) and presents abruptly.","2":"Amniotic fluid embolism occurs during labour or immediately postpartum.","4":"Septic emboli arise from infective endocarditis or infected thrombi, not acute long-bone fractures."}},{"id":70,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Neoplasia","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 63-year-old heavy smoker presents with cough, haemoptysis, polyuria and constipation. Chest X-ray shows a centrally located hilar mass. Serum calcium is 13.0 mg/dL with a suppressed parathyroid hormone level and no bone metastases. Which tumour is most likely?","options":["Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung","Small cell carcinoma of the lung","Adenocarcinoma of the lung","Bronchial carcinoid tumour","Large cell carcinoma of the lung"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Humoral hypercalcaemia of malignancy, the commonest paraneoplastic syndrome, is due to secretion of parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHrP); PTH itself is suppressed. Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung, typically central and strongly associated with smoking, is the classic cause. Small cell carcinoma is associated with SIADH and ectopic ACTH.","why_wrong":{"1":"Small cell carcinoma classically causes SIADH, ectopic ACTH and Lambert-Eaton syndrome rather than PTHrP-mediated hypercalcaemia.","2":"Adenocarcinoma is usually peripheral and is not the classic source of PTHrP.","3":"Carcinoid tumours may cause carcinoid syndrome or Cushing syndrome, not humoral hypercalcaemia.","4":"Large cell carcinoma is usually peripheral and is not classically linked to hypercalcaemia."}},{"id":71,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Neoplasia","domain":"knowledge","question":"High-risk human papillomavirus types 16 and 18 are implicated in carcinoma of the cervix. The viral E7 oncoprotein contributes to malignant transformation mainly by binding and inactivating which host protein?","options":["p53 protein","BCL-2 protein","RB protein","RAS protein","MYC protein"],"answer":2,"explanation":"HPV E7 binds the hypophosphorylated RB protein, releasing E2F transcription factors and driving cells through the G1/S checkpoint; it also inactivates the CDK inhibitors p21 and p27. HPV E6 binds p53 and promotes its ubiquitin-mediated degradation. Mnemonic: E6 targets p53, E7 targets RB ('6 before 7, 53 before RB').","why_wrong":{"0":"p53 is targeted for degradation by the HPV E6 protein, not E7.","1":"BCL-2 is an anti-apoptotic protein overexpressed in follicular lymphoma via t(14;18).","3":"RAS is a GTP-binding oncogene product activated by point mutations, not by HPV E7.","4":"MYC is a transcription factor oncogene activated by translocation in Burkitt lymphoma."}},{"id":72,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Neoplasia","domain":"knowledge","question":"A pathologist is asked which single feature most reliably establishes that a tumour is malignant rather than benign. Which of the following is it?","options":["High mitotic count","Lack of a fibrous capsule","Nuclear pleomorphism","Large size of the tumour","Presence of distant metastasis"],"answer":4,"explanation":"Of all the features distinguishing benign from malignant tumours (differentiation, rate of growth, local invasion, metastasis), metastasis unequivocally marks a tumour as malignant. Benign neoplasms do not metastasise. Exceptions to frequent metastasis among malignant tumours include basal cell carcinoma and gliomas, which are locally invasive but rarely metastasise.","why_wrong":{"0":"Mitoses can be numerous in benign tumours and normal proliferating tissues; atypical mitoses are more suggestive but not definitive.","1":"Several benign tumours, such as haemangiomas, are unencapsulated, so absence of a capsule is not diagnostic of malignancy.","2":"Pleomorphism can occur in benign lesions such as some benign tumours and reactive conditions.","3":"Size does not determine malignancy; benign tumours can grow very large."}},{"id":73,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Genetics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A couple who are first cousins attend a clinic before planning a family. Both have microcytic red cell indices with HbA2 of 5.2% on haemoglobin electrophoresis, consistent with beta-thalassaemia trait. What is the probability that any one of their children will have beta-thalassaemia major?","options":["0%","50%","75%","25%","100%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Beta-thalassaemia is autosomal recessive. When both parents are heterozygous carriers, each child has a 25% chance of being homozygous affected (thalassaemia major), 50% chance of being a carrier and 25% chance of being unaffected. Consanguineous marriage, very common in Pakistan, increases the frequency of such carrier matings, which is why premarital carrier screening (raised HbA2 >3.5%) is recommended.","why_wrong":{"0":"Both parents are carriers of an autosomal recessive gene, so an affected child is possible.","1":"50% is the probability that each child will be a heterozygous carrier like the parents.","2":"75% is the probability that a child will be phenotypically unaffected by thalassaemia major (normal or trait).","4":"Each pregnancy has a 1 in 4 chance, not certainty, of an affected child."}},{"id":74,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Immunology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 25-year-old woman develops an itchy, eczematous, vesicular rash on her earlobes 48 hours after wearing new artificial earrings containing nickel. Patch testing with nickel is positive at 48-72 hours. What is the mechanism of this reaction?","options":["IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation","Sensitised T lymphocytes releasing cytokines","IgG antibodies against fixed tissue antigens","Deposition of antigen-antibody complexes","Direct irritant damage to keratinocytes without sensitisation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Allergic contact dermatitis is a type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity: nickel acts as a hapten that binds self proteins, and previously sensitised CD4+ Th1 (and CD8+) T cells release cytokines that recruit macrophages, peaking at 48-72 hours. The patch test reproduces this delayed response. Other type IV examples include the tuberculin (Mantoux) test and granuloma formation.","why_wrong":{"0":"IgE-mediated (type I) reactions appear within minutes, e.g. urticaria and anaphylaxis.","2":"Antibody against fixed cell or tissue antigens is type II hypersensitivity.","3":"Immune complex deposition is type III hypersensitivity, e.g. serum sickness, Arthus reaction.","4":"Irritant contact dermatitis is non-immunological and does not give a positive delayed patch test."}},{"id":75,"subject":"Pathology","topic":"Immunology","domain":"knowledge","question":"During a renal transplant, within minutes of releasing the vascular clamps the donor kidney becomes cyanotic, mottled and flaccid and produces no urine. Biopsy shows widespread thrombosis of glomerular capillaries with neutrophils and fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Preformed recipient antibodies against donor endothelial antigens","Recipient CD8+ cytotoxic T cells against donor class I MHC","Donor T lymphocytes attacking recipient tissues","Intimal smooth muscle proliferation narrowing graft arteries","Recurrence of the original renal disease in the graft"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes to hours when the recipient has preformed antibodies (e.g. ABO isohaemagglutinins or anti-HLA antibodies from prior transfusion, pregnancy or transplant) against donor endothelium. Antibody binding activates complement, causing endothelial injury, platelet aggregation and thrombosis, so the graft never functions. It is now rare because of ABO matching and pre-transplant cross-matching.","why_wrong":{"1":"T-cell mediated acute cellular rejection develops after days to weeks, not minutes.","2":"This is graft-versus-host disease, typical of bone marrow transplantation.","3":"Progressive arterial intimal thickening is the hallmark of chronic rejection over months to years.","4":"Recurrent disease takes months to years and does not cause immediate graft thrombosis."}},{"id":76,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A drug has a volume of distribution of 40 L and an oral bioavailability of 0.5. The desired steady-state plasma concentration is 10 mg/L. What oral loading dose is required to achieve this concentration promptly?","options":["200 mg","400 mg","800 mg","1600 mg","20 mg"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Loading dose = (Target concentration x Vd) / F = (10 mg/L x 40 L) / 0.5 = 400 / 0.5 = 800 mg. A loading dose depends on volume of distribution, whereas the maintenance dose depends on clearance (maintenance dose rate = Cp x CL / F). Loading doses are useful for drugs with long half-lives, such as digoxin and amiodarone, where steady state would otherwise take 4-5 half-lives.","why_wrong":{"0":"200 mg would result from wrongly multiplying by bioavailability and halving the required amount.","1":"400 mg is the dose for intravenous administration (F = 1) and ignores the 50% oral bioavailability.","3":"1600 mg would double the dose twice, giving a plasma concentration of 20 mg/L.","4":"20 mg would come from dividing target concentration by bioavailability without multiplying by Vd."}},{"id":77,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","domain":"knowledge","question":"Two vasodilators, Drug X and Drug Y, are tested on isolated arterial strips. Drug X has an EC50 of 1 mg, Drug Y has an EC50 of 10 mg, and both produce the same maximal relaxation at high doses. Which statement is correct?","options":["Drug X is more efficacious than Drug Y and equally potent","Drug Y is more potent than Drug X but equally efficacious","Drug Y is a partial agonist with low intrinsic activity","Drug X has a wider therapeutic index than Drug Y","Drug X is more potent than Drug Y but equally efficacious"],"answer":4,"explanation":"Potency is the amount of drug needed to produce a given effect and is reflected by the EC50 (a lower EC50 means higher potency; the curve lies further to the left). Efficacy is the maximal effect a drug can produce regardless of dose (Emax). Clinically, efficacy is usually more important than potency, since a less potent drug can simply be given in a larger dose.","why_wrong":{"0":"Efficacy (Emax) is the same for both drugs, and their EC50 values, hence potencies, differ.","1":"Drug Y needs a tenfold higher dose for the same effect, so it is less potent.","2":"A partial agonist would have a lower maximal effect than a full agonist, which is not the case here.","3":"Therapeutic index requires toxic dose data (TD50/ED50), which is not given."}},{"id":78,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old farmer is brought in after spraying pesticide on a cotton crop. He has pinpoint pupils, profuse salivation and sweating, bronchorrhoea, bradycardia and muscle fasciculations. Besides atropine, which drug should be given early because it can regenerate the inhibited enzyme before 'ageing' occurs?","options":["Pralidoxime","Physostigmine","Neostigmine","Glycopyrrolate","Diazepam"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Organophosphates phosphorylate acetylcholinesterase, causing muscarinic (DUMBELS), nicotinic (fasciculations, weakness) and CNS effects. Atropine blocks muscarinic effects only, while pralidoxime (an oxime) removes the phosphate group and reactivates the enzyme, improving nicotinic muscle weakness. It must be given early, before the enzyme-phosphate bond 'ages' and becomes irreversible.","why_wrong":{"1":"Physostigmine is itself a cholinesterase inhibitor and would worsen poisoning.","2":"Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase and is contraindicated in organophosphate poisoning.","3":"Glycopyrrolate is a peripheral antimuscarinic; it does not reactivate cholinesterase or reverse nicotinic effects.","4":"Diazepam controls seizures and agitation but does not reactivate cholinesterase."}},{"id":79,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 38-year-old woman with paroxysmal headaches, palpitations and sweating has markedly raised urinary metanephrines and a 4 cm adrenal mass. She is being prepared for adrenalectomy. Which drug should be started FIRST to control her blood pressure?","options":["Propranolol","Atenolol","Clonidine","Phenoxybenzamine","Hydrochlorothiazide"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In phaeochromocytoma, alpha-adrenergic blockade with phenoxybenzamine (a non-selective, irreversible alpha-blocker) is started 10-14 days before surgery, along with liberal salt and fluid intake to restore volume. A beta-blocker is added only after alpha-blockade to control tachycardia. Beta-blockade alone removes beta2-mediated vasodilation and leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed.","why_wrong":{"0":"Starting a beta-blocker first leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed and can precipitate a hypertensive crisis.","1":"Even a cardioselective beta-blocker should only be added after adequate alpha-blockade.","2":"Clonidine reduces central sympathetic outflow but does not block catecholamines secreted by the tumour.","4":"Thiazides worsen the volume contraction of phaeochromocytoma and do not block catecholamine effects."}},{"id":80,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 20-year-old man with acute psychosis is started on intramuscular haloperidol. Thirty-six hours later he develops painful sustained twisting of the neck to one side, his eyes roll upwards and he has difficulty opening his mouth. Temperature and CK are normal. Which drug is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","options":["Dantrolene","Benztropine","Bromocriptine","Propranolol","Tetrabenazine"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Acute dystonia (torticollis, oculogyric crisis, trismus) occurs within hours to days of starting a high-potency D2 antagonist such as haloperidol, due to relative cholinergic excess in the striatum. It responds rapidly to parenteral anticholinergics such as benztropine or procyclidine (or diphenhydramine). Remember the timeline: dystonia (hours-days), akathisia (days-weeks), parkinsonism (weeks), tardive dyskinesia (months-years).","why_wrong":{"0":"Dantrolene is used for neuroleptic malignant syndrome and malignant hyperthermia, which cause fever and high CK.","2":"Bromocriptine is used in neuroleptic malignant syndrome, not acute dystonia.","3":"Propranolol is the treatment of choice for antipsychotic-induced akathisia.","4":"Tetrabenazine, a VMAT2 inhibitor, is used for tardive dyskinesia and chorea."}},{"id":81,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 28-year-old man who has taken an anti-epileptic drug for generalised tonic-clonic seizures for several years has swollen, overgrown gums, coarse facial features, hirsutism and megaloblastic anaemia. On examination he has nystagmus and an ataxic gait. Which drug is he most likely taking?","options":["Sodium valproate","Carbamazepine","Phenytoin","Ethosuximide","Levetiracetam"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Phenytoin's chronic adverse effects include gingival hyperplasia, hirsutism, coarsening of facial features, megaloblastic anaemia (folate deficiency), osteomalacia (enzyme induction of vitamin D metabolism) and teratogenicity (fetal hydantoin syndrome). Dose-related toxicity causes nystagmus, ataxia and diplopia. It follows zero-order (saturation) kinetics within the therapeutic range, so small dose increases can cause large rises in plasma levels.","why_wrong":{"0":"Valproate causes weight gain, tremor, hair loss, hepatotoxicity and neural tube defects, not gum hyperplasia.","1":"Carbamazepine causes diplopia, hyponatraemia (SIADH), agranulocytosis and SJS, not gingival hyperplasia.","3":"Ethosuximide is used only for absence seizures and mainly causes GI upset.","4":"Levetiracetam is notable for behavioural changes and irritability, not gingival hyperplasia."}},{"id":82,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Chemotherapy and Immunosuppressants","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 35-year-old woman receiving high-dose cyclophosphamide for lymphoma develops dysuria and frank haematuria. Cystoscopy shows diffuse mucosal haemorrhage. Which agent given with the chemotherapy would most likely have prevented this complication?","options":["Leucovorin (folinic acid)","Dexrazoxane","Ondansetron","Allopurinol","Mesna"],"answer":4,"explanation":"Cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide are metabolised to acrolein, which is excreted in urine and damages the bladder urothelium, causing haemorrhagic cystitis. Mesna (2-mercaptoethane sulfonate) is concentrated in urine, where its free sulfhydryl group binds and inactivates acrolein. Vigorous hydration and frequent voiding also help.","why_wrong":{"0":"Leucovorin rescues normal cells from methotrexate toxicity.","1":"Dexrazoxane is an iron chelator that protects against anthracycline (doxorubicin) cardiotoxicity.","2":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic and does not protect the bladder.","3":"Allopurinol prevents hyperuricaemia of tumour lysis syndrome, not haemorrhagic cystitis."}},{"id":83,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Pharmacology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 55-year-old diabetic hypertensive man started on enalapril two months ago complains of a persistent, dry, irritating cough. His chest X-ray and spirometry are normal. Accumulation of which substance is chiefly responsible for this side effect?","options":["Angiotensin II","Bradykinin","Aldosterone","Histamine","Renin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ACE (kininase II) degrades bradykinin; ACE inhibitors therefore cause bradykinin (and substance P) to accumulate in the airways, producing a dry cough in up to 10-20% of patients and, rarely, angioedema. Switching to an angiotensin receptor blocker such as losartan, which does not affect bradykinin metabolism, usually resolves the cough. ACE inhibitors are preferred in diabetics because they slow diabetic nephropathy.","why_wrong":{"0":"ACE inhibitors reduce, not increase, angiotensin II formation.","2":"Aldosterone secretion falls with ACE inhibition and does not cause cough.","3":"Histamine release is not the mechanism of ACE inhibitor cough.","4":"Plasma renin rises due to loss of feedback but does not cause cough."}},{"id":84,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Pharmacology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 66-year-old man has been taking an antiarrhythmic drug for recurrent ventricular tachycardia for 18 months. He now has progressive exertional dyspnoea with bilateral reticular shadowing on chest X-ray, a raised TSH, bluish-grey discoloration of sun-exposed skin and corneal microdeposits on slit-lamp examination. Which drug is responsible?","options":["Sotalol","Flecainide","Verapamil","Amiodarone","Digoxin"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Amiodarone is an iodine-rich class III antiarrhythmic with a very long half-life and wide tissue distribution. Its toxicities include pulmonary fibrosis, hypo- or hyperthyroidism, corneal microdeposits, blue-grey skin discoloration and photosensitivity, hepatotoxicity and peripheral neuropathy. Baseline and periodic chest X-ray, thyroid and liver function tests are recommended during therapy.","why_wrong":{"0":"Sotalol's main risk is QT prolongation and torsades de pointes; it has beta-blocking effects, not iodine-related toxicity.","1":"Flecainide is a class IC drug with proarrhythmic risk, especially after myocardial infarction.","2":"Verapamil causes constipation, bradycardia and AV block.","4":"Digoxin toxicity causes nausea, arrhythmias and yellow-green vision, not pulmonary fibrosis."}},{"id":85,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Endocrine Pharmacology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 26-year-old woman at 8 weeks of gestation is diagnosed with Graves' disease, with tremor, tachycardia and a free T4 well above the normal range. Which drug is preferred to control her hyperthyroidism at this stage of pregnancy?","options":["Propylthiouracil","Carbimazole","Radioactive iodine","Potassium iodide (Lugol's iodine)","Propranolol alone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Propylthiouracil is preferred in the first trimester because carbimazole/methimazole is associated with congenital anomalies (aplasia cutis, choanal and oesophageal atresia). Because PTU carries a risk of severe hepatotoxicity, guidelines recommend switching to carbimazole/methimazole from the second trimester. PTU also inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to T3.","why_wrong":{"1":"Carbimazole/methimazole is avoided in the first trimester because of embryopathy, e.g. aplasia cutis and choanal atresia.","2":"Radioiodine is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy as it ablates the fetal thyroid.","3":"Iodide is used briefly before thyroidectomy or in thyroid storm, not for maintenance in pregnancy, and can cause fetal goitre.","4":"Propranolol only controls adrenergic symptoms and does not reduce thyroid hormone synthesis."}},{"id":86,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 45-year-old man on the intensive phase of treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis complains of blurred vision. Examination shows reduced visual acuity in both eyes and inability to distinguish red from green on Ishihara charts. Which drug is most likely responsible?","options":["Isoniazid","Rifampicin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide","Streptomycin"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ethambutol causes dose-related retrobulbar (optic) neuritis presenting with reduced visual acuity, central scotoma and loss of red-green colour discrimination. It is usually reversible if the drug is stopped promptly, so baseline and periodic visual acuity and colour vision testing are advised, and it is used cautiously in young children who cannot report visual symptoms. Mnemonic: 'E' for Eye.","why_wrong":{"0":"Isoniazid causes peripheral neuropathy (preventable with pyridoxine) and hepatitis.","1":"Rifampicin causes orange-red body fluids, hepatitis, flu-like syndrome and enzyme induction.","3":"Pyrazinamide causes hepatotoxicity, hyperuricaemia and arthralgia.","4":"Streptomycin causes ototoxicity (vestibular) and nephrotoxicity."}},{"id":87,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 65-year-old man has received intravenous gentamicin for 12 days for Pseudomonas sepsis, together with intravenous furosemide for fluid overload. He now complains of tinnitus and unsteadiness, and his serum creatinine has risen. Gentamicin exerts its bactericidal effect by:","options":["Inhibiting DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV","Inhibiting peptidyl transferase on the 50S ribosomal subunit","Binding the 30S ribosomal subunit and causing misreading of mRNA","Binding the D-Ala-D-Ala terminus of cell wall precursors","Inhibiting bacterial dihydrofolate reductase"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aminoglycosides bind irreversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit, blocking formation of the initiation complex and causing misreading of mRNA, which produces faulty proteins and cell death. Their uptake is oxygen-dependent, so they are inactive against anaerobes; killing is concentration-dependent with a post-antibiotic effect, allowing once-daily dosing. Key toxicities are ototoxicity (vestibular and cochlear, potentiated by loop diuretics such as furosemide), nephrotoxicity (proximal tubular necrosis) and neuromuscular blockade, so levels and renal function must be monitored.","why_wrong":{"0":"DNA gyrase/topoisomerase IV inhibition is the mechanism of fluoroquinolones such as ciprofloxacin.","1":"Peptidyl transferase inhibition on the 50S subunit is the mechanism of chloramphenicol.","3":"Binding D-Ala-D-Ala to block cell wall synthesis is the mechanism of vancomycin.","4":"Dihydrofolate reductase inhibition is the mechanism of trimethoprim."}},{"id":88,"subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 5-day-old preterm neonate treated for suspected meningitis develops abdominal distension, vomiting, hypothermia, an ashen-grey colour, flaccidity and circulatory collapse. Which antibiotic is most likely responsible?","options":["Gentamicin","Chloramphenicol","Ampicillin","Ceftriaxone","Cotrimoxazole"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Grey baby syndrome results from accumulation of chloramphenicol in neonates, who have deficient hepatic glucuronyl transferase and immature renal excretion. It presents with vomiting, abdominal distension, hypothermia, ashen-grey cyanosis, hypotension and cardiovascular collapse. Chloramphenicol also causes dose-related bone marrow suppression and idiosyncratic aplastic anaemia.","why_wrong":{"0":"Gentamicin causes ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity, not cardiovascular collapse with grey skin.","2":"Ampicillin is safe in neonates and is a standard part of neonatal sepsis therapy.","3":"Ceftriaxone in neonates may displace bilirubin and precipitate with calcium, but does not cause grey baby syndrome.","4":"Sulfonamides in neonates risk kernicterus by displacing bilirubin from albumin."}},{"id":89,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Biostatistics","domain":"knowledge","question":"In a survey of adult women, haemoglobin levels are normally distributed with a mean of 12 g/dL and a standard deviation of 1 g/dL. Approximately what percentage of women will have a haemoglobin level ABOVE 14 g/dL?","options":["About 2.5%","About 5%","About 16%","About 32%","About 0.15%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a normal distribution, about 68% of values lie within mean +/- 1 SD, 95% within mean +/- 2 SD (more precisely 1.96 SD) and 99.7% within mean +/- 3 SD. 14 g/dL is mean + 2 SD, so about 5% of values lie beyond +/- 2 SD, split equally between the two tails, leaving about 2.5% above 14 g/dL. The normal curve is symmetrical, so mean, median and mode coincide.","why_wrong":{"1":"5% lies outside mean +/- 2 SD in both tails combined, not above +2 SD alone.","2":"About 16% lies above mean + 1 SD (13 g/dL).","3":"About 32% lies outside mean +/- 1 SD in both tails combined.","4":"About 0.15% lies above mean + 3 SD (15 g/dL)."}},{"id":90,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Biostatistics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A researcher randomises hypertensive patients into three groups receiving a low-salt diet, a DASH diet or a usual diet, and wants to compare the mean systolic blood pressure among the three groups after 12 weeks. Which statistical test is most appropriate?","options":["Unpaired (independent samples) t-test","Paired t-test","Chi-square test","One-way analysis of variance (ANOVA)","Pearson correlation coefficient"],"answer":3,"explanation":"ANOVA (F-test) compares the means of three or more independent groups of a normally distributed quantitative variable in a single test, keeping the overall type I error at the chosen alpha. If ANOVA is significant, post-hoc tests (e.g. Tukey) identify which groups differ. Running repeated t-tests between pairs increases the chance of a false-positive result.","why_wrong":{"0":"The t-test compares means of only two groups; multiple t-tests inflate the type I error.","1":"The paired t-test compares two related measurements, e.g. before and after in the same subjects.","2":"Chi-square compares proportions of categorical data, not means of a continuous variable.","4":"Correlation measures the strength of linear association between two continuous variables."}},{"id":91,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Biostatistics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A cohort study reports that the relative risk of colorectal cancer in people who frequently eat processed meat compared with those who do not is 1.4, with a 95% confidence interval of 0.9 to 2.1. Which is the correct interpretation?","options":["Processed meat significantly increases cancer risk by 40%","Processed meat has a significant protective effect","The association is not statistically significant at the 5% level","The p-value must be less than 0.01","The study proves that there is no association"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For ratio measures (RR, OR) the null value is 1. If the 95% confidence interval includes 1, the result is not statistically significant at alpha = 0.05 (p > 0.05). A wide interval suggests imprecision, often due to small sample size, so a real effect cannot be excluded. For difference measures (e.g. difference in means) the null value is 0.","why_wrong":{"0":"Although the point estimate is 1.4, the confidence interval includes 1 so the increase is not statistically significant.","1":"A protective effect would require the RR and its whole CI to be below 1.","3":"A 95% CI that includes the null value implies p > 0.05.","4":"A non-significant result does not prove absence of association; the study may lack power (type II error)."}},{"id":92,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Epidemiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"In a cohort study, 1,000 smokers and 2,000 non-smokers free of disease are followed for 10 years. Thirty smokers and 10 non-smokers develop lung cancer. What is the relative risk of lung cancer in smokers compared with non-smokers?","options":["3","5","25","0.17","6"],"answer":4,"explanation":"Incidence in smokers = 30/1000 = 0.03 (30 per 1,000); incidence in non-smokers = 10/2000 = 0.005 (5 per 1,000). Relative risk = 0.03/0.005 = 6, meaning smokers are six times as likely to develop lung cancer. Attributable risk = 30 - 5 = 25 per 1,000, and attributable risk percent = 25/30 = 83%. RR can be calculated directly only from cohort studies (and RCTs).","why_wrong":{"0":"3 is obtained by wrongly dividing 30 by 10 without accounting for the different group sizes.","1":"5 does not correspond to the ratio of incidences (30/1000 divided by 10/2000).","2":"25 per 1,000 is the attributable risk (incidence difference), not the relative risk.","3":"0.17 is the inverse ratio (non-smokers/smokers) and would wrongly suggest smoking is protective."}},{"id":93,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Epidemiology","domain":"knowledge","question":"During an outbreak of a communicable disease, healthy household contacts of a confirmed case are kept away from others and their movement restricted for a period equal to the longest usual incubation period of the disease. What is this measure called?","options":["Isolation","Segregation","Surveillance","Quarantine","Notification"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Quarantine (Park) is the limitation of freedom of movement of well persons or animals exposed to a communicable disease, for a period not longer than the longest usual incubation period, to prevent contact with those not so exposed. Isolation applies to infected persons for the duration of communicability. Modified quarantine and personal surveillance are less restrictive alternatives.","why_wrong":{"0":"Isolation is separation of infected persons (cases or carriers) during the period of communicability.","1":"Segregation is separation of a group of persons or animals for special consideration or observation, without the time limit of quarantine.","2":"Surveillance means close observation of contacts without restricting their movement.","4":"Notification is reporting of a case to the health authority."}},{"id":94,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Public Health Practice","domain":"knowledge","question":"A new rapid test for a disease is evaluated in 1,000 people: 200 have the disease by the gold standard and 800 do not. The test is positive in 180 of the diseased and negative in 760 of the non-diseased. What is the positive predictive value of the test in this population?","options":["82%","90%","95%","97%","18%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"From the 2x2 table: true positives = 180, false negatives = 20, true negatives = 760, false positives = 40. PPV = TP/(TP + FP) = 180/220 = 81.8%, i.e. about 82%. Sensitivity = 180/200 = 90%, specificity = 760/800 = 95%, NPV = 760/780 = 97.4%. Unlike sensitivity and specificity, PPV rises as disease prevalence rises.","why_wrong":{"1":"90% (180/200) is the sensitivity of the test.","2":"95% (760/800) is the specificity of the test.","3":"97% (760/780) is the negative predictive value.","4":"18% (40/220) is the proportion of positive results that are false positives (1 - PPV)."}},{"id":95,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Public Health Practice","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 42-year-old man has worked for 15 years in a cotton textile mill in Faisalabad. He complains of chest tightness, cough and breathlessness that are worst on the first day of the working week after the weekend holiday and improve as the week goes on. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Silicosis","Byssinosis","Bagassosis","Asbestosis","Farmer's lung"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Byssinosis is an occupational lung disease of workers exposed to cotton, flax or hemp dust, typically in the carding and blowing rooms of textile mills. The classic feature is 'Monday morning tightness' (chest tightness on the first day back at work), which can progress to chronic irreversible airflow obstruction. Prevention includes dust control, exhaust ventilation, respiratory protection and periodic medical examination.","why_wrong":{"0":"Silicosis follows inhalation of free silica in stone-crushing, sandblasting and mining, with nodular upper-zone fibrosis.","2":"Bagassosis is an extrinsic allergic alveolitis from mouldy sugarcane fibre (bagasse).","3":"Asbestosis causes progressive lower-zone fibrosis and pleural plaques, without a weekly pattern.","4":"Farmer's lung is hypersensitivity pneumonitis from thermophilic actinomycetes in mouldy hay."}},{"id":96,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Public Health Practice","domain":"knowledge","question":"An 18-month-old child is brought to a community nutrition programme. Weight-for-height Z-score is -2.5 SD, mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC) is 120 mm and there is no bilateral pitting oedema. The child is alert, has a good appetite and no medical complications. How should this child be classified according to WHO criteria?","options":["Severe acute malnutrition without complications","Severe acute malnutrition with complications","Moderate acute malnutrition","Normal nutritional status","Chronic malnutrition (stunting)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"WHO criteria for children 6-59 months: SAM is WHZ < -3 SD, or MUAC < 115 mm, or bilateral pitting oedema. MAM is WHZ between -3 and -2 SD, or MUAC 115 to <125 mm, without oedema. This child (WHZ -2.5, MUAC 120 mm) has MAM and is managed in a supplementary feeding programme; SAM without complications is managed in outpatient therapeutic programmes with RUTF.","why_wrong":{"0":"SAM requires WHZ < -3 SD, MUAC < 115 mm or bilateral pitting oedema.","1":"This requires SAM criteria plus poor appetite or medical complications needing inpatient care.","3":"WHZ below -2 SD and MUAC below 125 mm indicate wasting.","4":"Stunting is defined by low height-for-age, which is not provided here."}},{"id":97,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Research Methods and EBM","domain":"knowledge","question":"To study risk factors for Wilms tumour, researchers identify 100 children diagnosed with the tumour at a cancer hospital and 200 children of similar age without the tumour, and ask their mothers about pesticide exposure during pregnancy. Which measure of association is most appropriate for this study?","options":["Relative risk","Attributable risk","Prevalence ratio","Incidence rate","Odds ratio"],"answer":4,"explanation":"This is a case-control study: subjects are selected by disease status (cases and controls) and past exposure is compared. Because incidence cannot be measured, the odds ratio (ad/bc) is used; for a rare disease it approximates the relative risk. Case-control studies are suited to rare diseases but are prone to recall bias, e.g. mothers of affected children recalling exposures more thoroughly.","why_wrong":{"0":"Relative risk requires incidence, which can only be measured in cohort studies or trials.","1":"Attributable risk is the difference in incidence between exposed and unexposed and needs cohort data.","2":"Prevalence ratios are used in cross-sectional studies.","3":"Incidence cannot be calculated when subjects are selected on the basis of disease status."}},{"id":98,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Research Methods and EBM","domain":"knowledge","question":"A public health researcher lives within a community of transgender (khwaja sira) people in Lahore for eight months, participating in their daily life and observing their customs, beliefs and shared practices in order to describe how their culture shapes health-seeking behaviour. Which qualitative design is this?","options":["Phenomenology","Grounded theory","Participatory action research","Ethnography","Case study"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Ethnography studies the culture of a group (shared beliefs, behaviours and practices) through prolonged immersion and participant observation in its natural setting, supplemented by interviews and field notes. It originated in anthropology. By contrast, phenomenology focuses on lived experience, and grounded theory on building theory from data.","why_wrong":{"0":"Phenomenology explores the individual lived experience and meaning of a phenomenon, usually through in-depth interviews.","1":"Grounded theory aims to generate a new theory from data through iterative coding and constant comparison.","2":"PAR involves the community as co-researchers to bring about action and change.","4":"A case study is an in-depth analysis of a single bounded case or event."}},{"id":99,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Research Methods and EBM","domain":"knowledge","question":"A postgraduate trainee has completed a prospective cohort study on gestational diabetes and type 2 diabetes in later life and is preparing the manuscript. Which reporting guideline should she follow?","options":["STROBE","CONSORT","PRISMA","MOOSE","STARD"],"answer":0,"explanation":"STROBE (Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology) provides checklists for cohort, case-control and cross-sectional studies. Other key guidelines: CONSORT (RCTs), PRISMA (systematic reviews/meta-analyses), MOOSE (meta-analyses of observational studies), STARD (diagnostic accuracy), CARE (case reports) and COREQ (qualitative research). The EQUATOR Network collects these guidelines.","why_wrong":{"1":"CONSORT is the reporting guideline for randomised controlled trials.","2":"PRISMA is used for reporting systematic reviews and meta-analyses.","3":"MOOSE is used for meta-analyses of observational studies.","4":"STARD is used for diagnostic accuracy studies."}},{"id":100,"subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Research Methods and EBM","domain":"knowledge","question":"A study finds that people who drink more than five cups of tea a day have a higher incidence of lung cancer. Further analysis shows that heavy tea drinkers are also much more likely to smoke cigarettes, and when the analysis is stratified by smoking status, the association between tea and lung cancer disappears. What best explains the original association?","options":["Selection bias","Confounding","Recall bias","Effect modification","Observer bias"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A confounder is associated with the exposure, is an independent risk factor for the outcome and is not on the causal pathway; smoking here is linked to tea drinking and causes lung cancer. Confounding is controlled at the design stage by randomisation, restriction and matching, and at the analysis stage by stratification and multivariable analysis. If the association had differed in strength between smokers and non-smokers, that would indicate effect modification.","why_wrong":{"0":"Selection bias arises from how participants are recruited or retained, not from a third variable linked to both exposure and outcome.","2":"Recall bias is differential accuracy of remembered exposure between cases and controls.","3":"Effect modification means the association differs across strata; here it disappears in every stratum.","4":"Observer bias results from systematic differences in how investigators measure outcomes or exposures."}}]};
